OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026", "title": "Buffered comparison and stopping-band resolution in critical Ising", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "218", "family_title": "Conformal universality for weakly interacting and random-bond Ising models", "abstract_html": "We prove pointwise comparison and finite stopping-band approximation theorems for the critical nearest-neighbor Ising model on the square lattice. Arbitrary common pinned spins are allowed in the comparison: the likelihood ratio is controlled by a zero-field FK connection probability on the graph with those vertices deleted. For an interface exploration across a band of fixed positive width, finitely many regular signed barriers approximate every good conditional target law in total variation, uniformly over bounded observables and sufficiently fine meshes.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Finite-graph comparison — original Theorem 1.1, proved as Lemma 2.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Finite-graph comparison — original Theorem 1.1, proved as Lemma 2.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G=(V,E)\\) be a finite graph with couplings \\(K_e\\geq 0\\) and finite real fields \\(h_v\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Its Ising law is\n  \\[\n  \\mu(\\sigma)=Z^{-1}\\exp\\!\\left(\n  \\sum_{\\{u,v\\}\\in E}K_{uv}\\sigma_u\\sigma_v+\n  \\sum_{v\\in V}h_v\\sigma_v\\right).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(I,J,F\\subseteq V\\) be pairwise disjoint, with \\(I,J\\neq\\varnothing\\), and prescribe a common configuration \\(\\tau\\) on \\(F\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Prescribing a spin retains its incident interactions as fields on its neighbors. Deleting a vertex removes that vertex and all incident edges.</p><p class=\"li\">Form a comparison graph by deleting \\(F\\), separately contracting \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) to two vertices, and setting all fields to zero. Add parallel couplings after contraction.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(q_0\\) be the probability that the two contracted vertices are connected in the zero-field random-cluster model with cluster weight \\(2\\) and edge parameters \\(p_e=1-e^{-2K_e}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all configurations \\(a\\) on \\(I\\) and \\(b,b'\\) on \\(J\\),\n\\[\ne^{-4\\operatorname{arctanh}q_0}\n\\leq\n\\frac{\\mu(\\sigma_I=a\\mid \\sigma_J=b,\\sigma_F=\\tau)}\n{\\mu(\\sigma_I=a\\mid \\sigma_J=b',\\sigma_F=\\tau)}\n\\leq\ne^{4\\operatorname{arctanh}q_0}.\n\\]\nEither conditional law may be replaced by an arbitrary mixture of these conditional laws. The bound is symmetric in \\(I,J\\). Arbitrary finite external fields are allowed in the original law and are set to zero only in the comparator.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 1 after retaining prescribed-spin contributions as fields and deleting their vertices.</p><p>For fixed \\(b,b'\\), the oscillation over \\(a\\) of the logarithm of the conditional density ratio is at most\n\\[\nL\\leq4\\operatorname{arctanh}q_0.\n\\]\nThe ratio has mean one under its denominator law; hence its minimum is at most one and maximum at least one. Every ratio is therefore in \\([e^{-L},e^L]\\).</p><p>Average the pointwise inequality to allow a mixture in one law, then in the other. Symmetry follows from Proposition 1. This proves original Theorem 1.1 and Lemma 2.2.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Finite conditional resolution and stopping-band transfer — original Theorems 1.2 and 9.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Finite conditional resolution and stopping-band transfer — original Theorems 1.2 and 9.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work with the critical nearest-neighbor square-lattice Ising model at\n  \\[\n  \\beta_c=\\tfrac12\\log(1+\\sqrt2)\n  \\]\n  on \\(\\delta\\mathbb Z^2\\), with zero external field and prescribed boundary spins.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  Q_r=\\{z\\in\\mathbb R^2:\\|z\\|_\\infty&lt;r\\},\\qquad \\gamma_r=\\partial Q_r.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;u&lt;v\\), an ambient square chart, and a compact target with fixed positive clearance from the band \\(Q_v\\setminus Q_u\\) and the relevant ambient boundary.</p><p class=\"li\">In the inward construction, reveal the outside of \\(Q_v\\) and explore toward \\(\\gamma_u\\); the target is compactly contained in \\(Q_u\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In the outward construction, reveal \\(Q_u\\) and explore toward \\(\\gamma_v\\); the target lies between \\(\\gamma_v\\) and a fixed more distant square wall, with positive clearances from both. The distant wall remains unchanged during each comparison.</p><p class=\"li\">Permitted contour levels range over prescribed separated open windows whose closures preserve every required clearance.</p><p class=\"li\">Round each requested radius deterministically to the nearest element of \\(\\delta\\mathbb N\\). Include the starting-contour vertices in the initial reveal.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a diagonal in each lattice face and affinely interpolate spins on the resulting triangles. Explore every zero-set strand meeting the starting contour until its first return to that contour or first hit of the other contour, revealing traversed triangles’ spins. The complete adaptive reveal is the stopping transcript.</p><p class=\"li\">A regular signed barrier is a deterministic polygonal or smooth Jordan contour with finitely many prescribed plus and minus arcs, together with a separating lattice boundary layer following those arcs. Its fresh side contains the target.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the critical plus-boundary law in one fixed larger reference square.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\|\\nu-\\nu'\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}=\\sup_A|\\nu(A)-\\nu'(A)|.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\epsilon,\\eta&gt;0\\), generic contour levels can be chosen in any prescribed separated subwindows, and there exist a finite descriptor\n\\[\nZ^\\delta\\in\\{0,1,\\ldots,m\\},\n\\]\ncomputed from band spins, and regular signed barriers \\(\\Gamma_1,\\ldots,\\Gamma_m\\), such that for all sufficiently fine meshes:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(\\mathbb P(Z^\\delta=0)\\leq\\epsilon\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For every realizable transcript in state \\(i\\geq1\\), insert the plus pins of \\(\\Gamma_i\\), then its minus pins. The sum of the two target total-variation changes is at most \\(\\eta\\). The completed target-side law \\(Q_{i,\\delta}\\) is independent of the transcript within state \\(i\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The conclusion holds simultaneously for all bounded target observables, including mesh-dependent observables.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. In the inward case, the completed barrier may additionally be moved to a slightly farther signed contour while retaining the entire enclosed free component as the kept volume. Including the two old-pin removals, the total target error can still be at most \\(\\eta\\). The final kept volume has a positive macroscopic buffer from every fixed pin.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. The barriers, the number of states, and all positive geometric clearances are selected before the mesh threshold; their clearances have positive minima over the good states.</p><p class=\"li num\">6. For any fixed finite list of bands, descriptors may be chosen with convergent joint laws. Their bins are continuity sets of the limiting data law, so this finite-space convergence is in total variation.</p><p class=\"li num\">7. If another law has band density at most \\(K\\) times the reference band density, its exceptional probability is at most \\(K\\epsilon\\). This also holds for mixtures of such conditional laws.</p><p>Only the exceptional probability transfers from a band-density bound alone; identifying a new conditional target law requires a common continuation beyond a source contour.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose generic levels by Proposition 8. At every admissible limiting datum apply Proposition 12, including its barrier-displacement part inwardly, allocating the requested error budget among the ordered steps.</p><p>This gives an open neighborhood and one barrier construction valid for every realizable transcript in that neighborhood below a datum-dependent mesh threshold.</p><p>Refine the cover to balls whose closures lie in these neighborhoods and whose boundaries have zero limiting probability. For a fixed center, only countably many radii have positive sphere mass. Separability from Proposition 8 gives a countable subcover.</p><p>Choose finitely many balls whose union has limiting probability greater than \\(1-\\epsilon/2\\). Order them and assign each datum to the first containing ball, producing disjoint good bins. Every bin boundary is contained in the finite union of zero-mass sphere boundaries. The remaining bin is exceptional.</p><p>Data convergence gives exceptional probability at most \\(\\epsilon\\) for all sufficiently fine meshes. Associate each good bin with its containing neighborhood’s barrier. Proposition 12 applies uniformly to every transcript in the bin and independently of the observable. Take minima of the finitely many positive clearances and mesh thresholds.</p><p>For a fixed finite list of bands, use Proposition 8 jointly. Every finite descriptor string is a continuity set, giving convergence of all its probabilities, hence total-variation convergence on the finite state space.</p><p>The exceptional event depends only on band spins. A band-density bound \\(K\\) therefore multiplies its probability by at most \\(K\\), also for mixtures. It does not itself change the reference continuation into another continuation. This proves the full original Theorem 9.1 and its introductory formulation, Theorem 1.2.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Nearby-contour magnetizations and prediction — original Proposition 5.1 and Corollary 5.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Nearby-contour magnetizations and prediction — original Proposition 5.1 and Corollary 5.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work at criticality in lattice units.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(J_s,J_s'\\) be parallel horizontal or vertical lattice segments of lengths \\(O(s)\\). Their matching portions differ by a displacement of length at most \\(Cw\\), and their unmatched end pieces have total length \\(O(w)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Alternatively, let them be concentric square contours of radii comparable to \\(s\\), with radius difference at most \\(Cw\\), matching corresponding sides.</p><p class=\"li\">Their union has a pin-free square-lattice neighborhood of width \\(cs\\), covered by a fixed number of buffered planar charts. Pins beyond that neighborhood may have arbitrary signs, and mixtures of these laws are allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  M_{J_s}=s^{-7/8}\\sum_{x\\in J_s}\\sigma_x,\\qquad\n  M_{J_s'}=s^{-7/8}\\sum_{x\\in J_s'}\\sigma_x.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Hold \\(h=w/s\\in(0,h_0]\\) fixed while \\(s\\to\\infty\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a constant \\(C_0\\), depending only on the fixed geometry and buffer, such that\n\\[\n\\limsup_{s\\to\\infty}\\|M_{J_s}-M_{J_s'}\\|_2\n\\leq C_0h^{3/8}.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains the spins on \\(J_s'\\), then\n\\[\n\\limsup_{s\\to\\infty}\n\\|M_{J_s}-\\mathbb E[M_{J_s}\\mid\\mathcal F]\\|_2\n\\leq C_0(w/s)^{3/8}.\n\\]\nThe normalization remains \\(s^{-7/8}\\), even for contours of different lengths.</p><p>The same conclusions hold for nearby full periodic rows of period \\(W\\), when \\(W/s\\) is fixed and positive; constants may depend on \\(W/s\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Proposition 2 on the nonnegative square of each local line difference to reduce buffered charts to the whole-plane law at a fixed factor. A finite chart decomposition and the \\(L^2\\) triangle inequality suffice.</p><p>The whole-plane mean is zero. Proposition 3 gives convergence of normalized two-point sums to integrals of\n\\[\nK(x-y)=c_\\sigma|x-y|^{-1/4}.\n\\]\nFor varying endpoint data, pass first to a subsequence realizing the upper limit, then to convergent rescaled endpoint and length data, after a common translation. These data range over a compact set.</p><p>To control the diagonal, the uniform two-point bound shows that pairs at rescaled distance at most \\(r\\) contribute at most\n\\[\nCr^{3/4}+o(1).\n\\]\nEqual-site terms are \\(O(s^{-3/4})\\). Away from the diagonal, uniform directional asymptotics justify ordinary Riemann sums, including cross terms.</p><p>Pair matching segment points by displacement \\(v\\), \\(|v|\\leq Ch\\). In the limiting squared difference, pairs of parameters at distance at most \\(2Ch\\) contribute\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(\\int_0^{C'h}r^{-1/4}\\,dr\\right)=O(h^{3/4}),\n\\]\nincluding nearby shifted singularities.</p><p>For the far part,\n\\[\n|\\nabla K(u)|\\leq C|u|^{-5/4}.\n\\]\nThe mean value theorem bounds the combined kernel difference by \\(Ch|u|^{-5/4}\\), whose integral is\n\\[\nCh\\int_{2Ch}^{C'}r^{-5/4}\\,dr=O(h^{3/4}).\n\\]\nThus matching portions have squared norm \\(O(h^{3/4})\\).</p><p>An unmatched end piece of length \\(O(h)\\) has second moment \\(O(h^{7/4})\\), hence norm \\(O(h^{7/8})\\). There are finitely many pieces, and \\(h^{7/8}\\leq h^{3/8}\\) for \\(h\\leq1\\). This proves the segment estimate.</p><p>For squares, trim corresponding sides to equal lengths and treat matching translated portions and short remainders. For periodic rows, subdivide at fixed common locations into segments of length a sufficiently small fixed fraction of \\(W\\); each matching pair and its buffer lies in a planar chart. Decrease \\(h_0\\) if necessary depending on \\(W/s\\), then sum finitely many segment norms.</p><p>Uniform density comparison permits arbitrary exterior pins and mixtures.</p><p>Finally \\(M_{J_s'}\\) is \\(\\mathcal F\\)-measurable. Conditional expectation minimizes \\(L^2\\) distance, so\n\\[\n\\|M_{J_s}-\\mathbb E[M_{J_s}\\mid\\mathcal F]\\|_2\n\\leq\\|M_{J_s}-M_{J_s'}\\|_2.\n\\]\nThe relative displacement is fixed before the mesh limit; no discrete derivative estimate is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Lookup approximation of conditional averages — original Corollary 9.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Lookup approximation of conditional averages — original Corollary 9.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the outward setting, stopping information \\(\\mathcal F^\\delta\\), descriptor \\(Z^\\delta\\), and completed laws \\(Q_{i,\\delta}\\) of Theorem 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(|h_\\delta|\\leq H\\), where \\(h_\\delta\\) is any target observable.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  a_i(\\delta)=\\mathbb E_{Q_{i,\\delta}}h_\\delta\\qquad(i\\geq1),\n  \\]\n  and choose \\(a_0(\\delta)\\in[-H,H]\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On every good transcript,\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\mathbb E[h_\\delta\\mid\\mathcal F^\\delta]\n-a_{Z^\\delta}(\\delta)\n\\right|\\leq2H\\eta.\n\\]\nIn the reference chart,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\left|\n\\mathbb E[h_\\delta\\mid\\mathcal F^\\delta]\n-a_{Z^\\delta}(\\delta)\n\\right|^2\n\\leq4H^2(\\eta^2+\\epsilon).\n\\]</p><p>The same bins, barriers, and mesh threshold work simultaneously for all such observables. Neither the observables nor their lookup values need converge.</p><p>More precisely, fix a reference continuation law \\(\\nu\\) and define, as a function of the stopping transcript \\(\\mathcal T\\),\n\\[\nf_\\delta^\\nu(\\mathcal T)\n=\\mathbb E_\\nu[h_\\delta\\mid\\mathcal F^\\delta=\\mathcal T].\n\\]\nEvaluating this same function under a buffered band distribution with density bound \\(K\\) gives the integrated estimate with exceptional term \\(K\\epsilon\\). It equals the new law’s conditional average only when that law has the same continuation and distant wall given \\(\\mathcal T\\).</p><p>For a changed distant-wall assignment \\(b\\), the uniform wall extension supplies completed laws \\(Q^b_{i,\\delta}\\), with separate lookup values\n\\[\na_i^b(\\delta)=\\mathbb E_{Q^b_{i,\\delta}}h_\\delta.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 8, a transcript’s conditional target law is its Gibbs continuation. Theorem 2 puts it within total variation \\(\\eta\\) of \\(Q_{i,\\delta}\\) on each good bin. Hence every \\(|h_\\delta|\\leq H\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\mathbb E[h_\\delta\\mid\\mathcal F^\\delta]-a_i(\\delta)\n\\right|\\leq2H\\eta.\n\\]</p><p>On the exceptional bin both quantities are bounded by \\(H\\), so the difference is at most \\(2H\\). Squaring and integrating gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\left|\n\\mathbb E[h_\\delta\\mid\\mathcal F^\\delta]\n-a_{Z^\\delta}(\\delta)\n\\right|^2\n\\leq4H^2(\\eta^2+\\epsilon).\n\\]</p><p>All choices in Theorem 2 precede the observable. The pointwise good-bin estimate and transferred exceptional probability give the assertion for the same reference conditional-average function evaluated under another band distribution.</p><p>Equality with the new conditional average requires the same continuation and distant wall. For a changed prescribed wall, Proposition 12 supplies the corresponding completed laws and therefore the separate values \\(a_i^b(\\delta)\\). No continuum limit of the observables or lookup values is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Random levels and finite ball probes — original Corollary 9.3.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Random levels and finite ball probes — original Corollary 9.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the geometries and positive clearances of Theorem 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Draw the two contour radii with bounded density in a fixed compact rectangle of separated radii strictly inside the permitted windows. Different band observations may use independent draws.</p><p class=\"li\">Include the radius draws in the stopping information.</p><p class=\"li\">A facing datum consists of an oriented Jordan base with finitely many disjoint attached signed slits, marked roots and tips, and the side signs specified in Proposition 8 below.</p><p class=\"li\">Within a fixed combinatorial type, measure distance by maximum uniform distance modulo increasing parametrizations, respecting orientation, attachments, marks, and side labels; cap the distance at \\(1\\), and assign distance \\(1\\) to different types.</p><p class=\"li\">The ambient space \\(\\widehat X\\) permits finite marked continuous walks of these types without simplicity, disjointness, or exact contour-incidence requirements.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a countable dense collection of marked rational polygonal-walk prototypes in \\(\\widehat X\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A probe tests whether the datum is within distance \\(r\\) of one prototype, where \\(r\\) is sampled independently and uniformly in a rational interval with closure in \\((0,1)\\). Probe balls are intersected with the admissible-data space.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Theorem 2 and the lookup conclusions of Theorem 4 remain valid for these random levels. Joint data laws converge, including the auxiliary randomness.</p><p>For every \\(\\epsilon,\\eta&gt;0\\), finitely many such probes suffice for the lookup approximation, with adjusted exceptional probability at most \\(\\epsilon\\). The probe list depends only on the reference local geometry, radius rectangle, and tolerances; it is independent of the bounded observables and of the size of an ambient buffered array.</p>", "proof": "<p>For almost every radius pair, Proposition 8’s deterministic continuity argument applies. Couple radius draws identically across meshes and independently of the converging loop collections. Their compact rectangle gives uniform positive contour separation and clearance from window edges. The deterministic rounding is measurable, and the recording maps have uniformly vanishing displacement.</p><p>The continuous mapping theorem, or dominated convergence under this coupling, gives joint convergence of encoded data and auxiliary radius draws. The full stopping information includes those draws. Proposition 12 gives a neighborhood for every resulting admissible datum, so the finite-cover proof of Theorem 2 and the lookup proof of Theorem 4 apply.</p><p>For finite probes, choose for every datum \\(d\\) a local-erosion neighborhood \\(U_d\\). Density of ambient prototypes gives a prototype \\(p\\) and rational \\(0&lt;a&lt;b&lt;1\\) with\n\\[\nd\\in B(p,a),\\qquad B(p,b)\\subseteq U_d.\n\\]\nChoose a rational threshold interval strictly between \\(a,b\\).</p><p>A positive probe certifies membership in \\(U_d\\), while every datum in \\(B(p,a)\\) tests positive for every threshold in that interval. These inner balls cover the admissible-data space. Select finitely many covering reference probability at least \\(1-\\epsilon/2\\).</p><p>Decode by the first positive test; if none is positive, use the exceptional state. Each positive test uses the single barrier law attached to its neighborhood, so the good-bin estimate is independent of the random threshold and the observable.</p><p>Conditional on the data, an independent continuous threshold has zero tie probability. Thus the finite bit string converges in law, and the exceptional probability is at most \\(\\epsilon\\) for fine meshes.</p><p>Only finitely many entries of a fixed dense enumeration are used. For finitely many allowed radius windows, take the union of their lists. The construction is made in one fixed local reference chart; Proposition 2 transfers exceptional-probability bounds to other buffered chart laws with the fixed density constant, independently of the number of tiles in a larger array. Conditional-law identification retains the common-continuation distinction of Theorem 4.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Extreme log-odds rectangles and balanced terminal interactions — original Lemmas 2.1–2.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Extreme log-odds rectangles and balanced terminal interactions — original Lemmas 2.1–2.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p(a,b)&gt;0\\) be the joint marginal on disjoint nonempty terminal sets \\(I,J\\) of a finite ferromagnetic Ising law with arbitrary finite fields.</p><p class=\"li\">Common prescribed spins on a disjoint set \\(F\\) are first incorporated as neighboring fields, and \\(F\\) is then removed.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(f=\\log p\\), and let \\(+\\) and \\(-\\) denote the constant configurations on the relevant terminal set.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  L=f(+,+)+f(-,-)-f(+,-)-f(-,+).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Separately contract \\(I,J\\), set every field to zero, and let \\(q_0\\) be the FK connection probability between the contracted terminals.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all \\(a,a',b,b'\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\nf(a,b)+f(a',b')-f(a,b')-f(a',b)\n\\right|\n\\leq L\\leq4\\operatorname{arctanh}q_0.\n\\]\nThe extreme rectangle and \\(q_0\\) are unchanged by interchanging \\(I,J\\). Oppositely signed common pins are deleted in the comparator, not wired together.</p>", "proof": "<p>Prescribed common spins contribute fields on surviving neighbors; remove their vertices after retaining those fields. Lemma 1.1 bounds every rectangle by the extreme rectangle \\(L\\). Restricting the terminal configurations to constant signs is their separate contraction, so Lemma 1.2 bounds \\(L\\) by \\(4\\operatorname{arctanh}q_0\\).</p><p>Both the extreme rectangle and the contracted connection event are symmetric in the two terminal sets.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Ferromagnetic marginal closure and extreme rectangles — original Lemma 2.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Ferromagnetic marginal closure and extreme rectangles — original Lemma 2.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the marginal \\(p\\), logarithm \\(f\\), and extreme rectangle \\(L\\) of Proposition 1. A positive weight \\(p\\) is log-supermodular when\n\\[\np(x\\wedge y)p(x\\vee y)\\geq p(x)p(y)\n\\]\nfor coordinatewise minimum and maximum.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The marginal \\(p\\) is log-supermodular, \\(L\\geq0\\), and every log-odds rectangle has absolute value at most \\(L\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The Ising weight is log-supermodular: each ferromagnetic pair interaction has nonnegative mixed difference, and each field has zero mixed difference.</p><p>To check preservation under summing one binary coordinate, denote the eight unsummed weights by \\(a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h\\), where \\((a,b,c,d)\\) and \\((e,f,g,h)\\) are the two square slices, in order \\((--,+-,-+,++)\\). Log-supermodularity gives\n\\[\nad\\geq bc,\\quad eh\\geq fg,\\quad ah\\geq bg,\\quad ah\\geq cf,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(ah)(ed)=(ad)(eh)\\geq(bg)(cf).\n\\]\nFor positive \\(X,Y,U,V\\), the conditions \\(X\\geq\\max(U,V)\\), \\(XY\\geq UV\\) imply \\(X+Y\\geq U+V\\): assuming \\(U\\geq V\\), use\n\\[\nX+\\frac{UV}{X}\\geq U+V\\qquad(X\\geq U).\n\\]\nApply this with \\((X,Y,U,V)=(ah,ed,bg,cf)\\). Thus\n\\[\nah+ed\\geq bg+cf,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(a+e)(d+h)\\geq(b+f)(c+g).\n\\]\nNonnegative adjacent-coordinate square differences are equivalent to log-supermodularity on a Boolean cube, by summing along coordinate paths. Iterating proves marginal closure.</p><p>For ordered \\(u\\leq v\\), \\(s\\leq t\\), put\n\\[\n\\Delta(u,v;s,t)\n=f(u,s)+f(v,t)-f(u,t)-f(v,s)\\geq0.\n\\]\nFor\n\\[\nD=f(a,b)+f(a',b')-f(a,b')-f(a',b),\n\\]\nthe exact identity\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL-D={}&amp;\\Delta(-,a';-,b)+\\Delta(a,+;b',+)\\\\\n&amp;+\\Delta(a',+;-,b')+\\Delta(-,a;b,+)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nproves \\(D\\leq L\\). Interchanging \\(b,b'\\) gives \\(-D\\leq L\\). No order between \\(a,a'\\) or \\(b,b'\\) is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Balancing terminal fields — the two-spin step in original Lemma 2.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Balancing terminal fields — the two-spin step in original Lemma 2.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Restrict each terminal set to a constant sign, equivalently contracting it to one vertex.</p><p class=\"li\">Write the resulting two-spin marginal weights as\n  \\[\n  w(s,t)=\\exp(C+Kst+As+Bt),\\qquad s,t\\in\\{-1,+1\\},\n  \\]\n  where \\(K=L/4\\geq0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\tanh K\\leq q_0,\n\\qquad\\text{and hence}\\qquad\nL\\leq4\\operatorname{arctanh}q_0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Add external fields \\(-A,-B\\) at the contracted terminals. Their marginal becomes proportional to \\(e^{Kst}\\), without changing \\(K\\). Both means vanish, so their covariance is \\(\\tanh K\\).</p><p>The arbitrary-field covariance inequality of Ding–Song–Sun, cited in the paper as [5, Corollary 1.3], applies to this finite ferromagnetic graph with arbitrary mixed-sign fields. It yields\n\\[\n\\tanh K\\leq\\langle\\sigma_I\\sigma_J\\rangle_0=q_0.\n\\]\nThe equality is the zero-field Edwards–Sokal identity. Internal terminal edges contribute constants, and parallel edges retain nonnegative summed coupling. Thus all hypotheses are preserved.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Critical buffered, small-patch, and cross-domain comparisons — original Lemma 2.4 and Corollaries 2.5–2.6.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Critical buffered, small-patch, and cross-domain comparisons — original Lemma 2.4 and Corollaries 2.5–2.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work on the square lattice at \\(\\beta_c\\), with\n  \\[\n  B_r=[-r,r]^2\\cap\\mathbb Z^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Compare conditional spin laws obtained by changing prescribed spins on \\(J\\), observing spins on \\(I\\), and retaining arbitrary common pins off \\(I\\cup J\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For cross-domain comparisons, conditioning beyond a common chart must produce the same local interactions and pinning rule.</p><p class=\"li\">A chart may be clipped by an agreeing free or fixed physical boundary and may contain deleted vertices or missing edges.</p><p class=\"li\">The symbol \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\) below denotes the exponent written \\(\\delta\\) in original equation (2.3), not a lattice mesh.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. For each \\(c&gt;0\\), if\n   \\[\n   I\\subseteq z+B_R,\\qquad\n   J\\subseteq(z+B_{(1+c)R})^c,\n   \\]\n   pointwise density ratios lie in \\([K_c^{-1},K_c]\\), where \\(K_c&lt;\\infty\\) depends only on \\(c\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. There are absolute \\(K,\\alpha&gt;0\\) such that, if\n   \\[\n   I\\subseteq z+B_w,\\qquad\n   J\\subseteq(z+B_R)^c,\\qquad R\\geq8w\\geq8,\n   \\]\n   every log-density ratio has absolute value at most\n   \\[\n   K(w/R)^\\alpha.\n   \\]\n   Consequently, total variation is at most \\(K'(w/R)^\\alpha\\), and a bounded test function \\(g\\) has expectation difference at most\n   \\[\n   2K'\\|g\\|_\\infty(w/R)^\\alpha.\n   \\]\n   No free neighborhood of the source patch is required.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. These comparisons hold between different domains, including plane, torus, or cylinder laws, whenever the common collar is an isometric square-lattice chart and all differing data lie beyond it.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Applying the same conditional randomization kernel preserves the comparisons, including internal Edwards–Sokal bond sampling.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. If the observed set is partitioned into at most \\(m\\) patches, each with a chart separated from every changed datum by a fixed positive relative distance, the full observed-law comparison constant is the product of the patch constants.</p><p class=\"li num\">6. The same conclusion holds for a partition of the changed source into at most \\(m\\) patches, each separated from the entire observed set. Polygonal and rotated collars are allowed through such finite covers.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a finite conditional graph, Proposition 1 bounds the oscillation of the log-density ratio by \\(4\\operatorname{arctanh}q_0\\). The ratio has mean one under its denominator law, so its minimum is at most one and maximum at least one. Thus it lies between the exponentials of plus and minus this oscillation bound. Averaging preserves pointwise inequalities for mixtures.</p><p>A fixed-ratio annulus separating the terminals contains a dual circuit with probability at least \\(c_1(c)&gt;0\\). Cover a middle subannulus by finitely many overlapping rectangles, require dual crossings and crossings in overlap squares, and use Lemma 2.1 and association. The resulting actual dual circuit separates the terminals even after their separate internal wiring. Common pins are deleted in the comparator; restoring them only increases connectivity. Hence\n\\[\nq_0\\leq1-c_1(c),\n\\]\nwhich gives the buffered comparison.</p><p>Between \\(B_{2w}\\) and \\(B_{R/2}\\), take disjoint annuli of radius ratio four. Their number is at least\n\\[\nc_2\\log(R/w)-c_3.\n\\]\nA terminal connection avoids every dual circuit, so the conditional tests give\n\\[\nq_0\\leq C_1(w/R)^{\\alpha_1}.\n\\]\nA fixed intervening annulus also gives \\(q_0\\leq1-c_4\\). On this interval,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{arctanh}q\\leq C_2q.\n\\]\nThis proves the small-patch logarithmic bound. Integrating the density difference gives the total-variation and bounded-observable bounds.</p><p>For clipped or incomplete charts, restore missing vertices and edges in the full square test rectangles after deleting common physical-boundary pins in the comparator. Restoration increases connectivity. No interior terminal identification is removed, so the same dual tests apply.</p><p>For different domains, condition at the common chart’s exterior neighbor spins. Both local laws are mixtures of the same finite-volume conditional kernels. Apply the mixture bound. Uniform finite-volume approximation gives the plane and cylinder statements. A common Markov kernel preserves pointwise domination.</p><p>For an observed-patch cover, reveal patches in order and factor configuration probabilities by the chain rule. Previously revealed patches are arbitrary common pins. Multiply the patch bounds.</p><p>For a source-patch cover, telescope the changed source patches while treating the others as common pins. The terminal symmetry of Proposition 1 places the small source patch in the inner terminal. Mixtures and common randomizations preserve the resulting bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Conditional crossing tests — original Lemma 2.4.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Conditional crossing tests — original Lemma 2.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(A\\geq1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Consider a critical FK-Ising rectangle whose side lengths are at least one and whose aspect ratio lies in \\([A^{-1},A]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Crossings use actual lattice edges, not abstract boundary identifications.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Under every boundary wiring, either specified open crossing and either specified dual crossing each have probability at least \\(c_A&gt;0\\). Bounded lattice corrections only change the constant.</p><p>A decreasing dual-crossing test has the same lower bound in a vertex-deleted graph, conditional on bonds outside its full rectangular test region, provided no terminal identification has vertices in the region’s interior. Additional deletions preserve the bound.</p><p>Uniform conditional bounds multiply for finitely many tests with disjoint unexposed edge sets; the corresponding upper bounds for simultaneous failures multiply as well.</p>", "proof": "<p>The rectangle assertion is critical FK-Ising RSW, cited as [6, arXiv version 1, Theorem 1], together with planar duality.</p><p>Conditioning exterior bonds produces a boundary wiring by the domain Markov property. Restore deleted vertices and edges in the test rectangle and wire its outer boundary. The resulting law dominates the original conditional bond law, so a decreasing dual event is at least as likely in the original law.</p><p>This domination follows from the single-edge conditional probabilities\n\\[\np_c,\\qquad \\frac{p_c}{p_c+2(1-p_c)},\n\\]\naccording as the endpoints are or are not already connected. Adding edges or wiring increases these probabilities. The terminal hypothesis prevents discarding an interior identification.</p><p>Order disjoint test regions. Conditioning outside the next region includes previous test results; apply the uniform conditional bound and average. Induction gives the product bounds.</p><p>If bounded lattice corrections leave a side of bounded length, finitely many prescribed open or closed edges provide a uniform replacement test by the same single-edge probabilities.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Static correlations and buffered magnetic moments — original Lemmas 3.1–3.3.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Static correlations and buffered magnetic moments — original Lemmas 3.1–3.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work with the critical nearest-neighbor Ising model, without external fields except those induced by pins.</p><p class=\"li\">Constants depend only on specified fixed bulk separations, aspect ratios, and buffers.</p><p class=\"li\">For the moment statement, fix \\(A,c&gt;0\\), \\(p\\geq1\\), and \\(\\nu\\in\\{1,2\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) have diameter at most \\(As\\), satisfy\n  \\[\n  |S\\cap B_t(x)|\\leq A(t+1)^\\nu,\\qquad |S|\\leq As^\\nu,\n  \\]\n  and possess a pin-free square-lattice neighborhood of width \\(cs\\). Pins beyond it may have arbitrary signs.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. Under any FK boundary wiring in a square of radius \\(r\\), for \\(x\\) in a fixed central subsquare,\n   \\[\n   cr^{-1/8}\\leq\\phi(x\\leftrightarrow\\partial B_r)\\leq Cr^{-1/8}.\n   \\]\n   Under plus boundary this is the corresponding magnetization estimate.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. In a plus rectangle of scale \\(r\\) and fixed aspect ratio, for points in separated compact bulk sets,\n   \\[\n   cr^{-1/4}\\leq\\operatorname{Cov}_+(\\sigma_x,\\sigma_y)\n   \\leq Cr^{-1/4}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The infinite-volume zero-field law on \\(\\mathbb Z^2\\) is unique and spin-flip symmetric. For a lattice-dependent \\(c_\\sigma&gt;0\\),\n   \\[\n   \\mathbb E(\\sigma_x\\sigma_y)\n   =c_\\sigma|x-y|^{-1/4}(1+o(1))\n   \\]\n   uniformly in direction as \\(|x-y|\\to\\infty\\), and\n   \\[\n   0\\leq\\mathbb E(\\sigma_x\\sigma_y)\n   \\leq C(1+|x-y|)^{-1/4}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. For \\(|a_x|\\leq1\\),\n   \\[\n   \\mathbb E\\left|\\sum_{x\\in S}a_x\\sigma_x\\right|^{2p}\n   \\leq C_{p,A,c}s^{2p(\\nu-1/8)}.\n   \\]\n   This includes finitely many line segments or square contours when \\(\\nu=1\\), and area sums when \\(\\nu=2\\), also in buffered torus or cylinder charts. No spin on \\(S\\) is pinned.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 3.1 gives items 1–3. For the moment estimate, use Proposition 2 and a fixed finite cover to compare local marginals to plus squares with proportional buffers.</p><p>Expand the even power and take absolute values. Lemma 3.2 and the diameter bound reduce the sum to a constant times\n\\[\n\\sum_{x_1,\\ldots,x_{2p}\\in S}\n\\prod_{i=1}^{2p}\\sum_{j\\ne i}\n(1+\\|x_i-x_j\\|_\\infty)^{-1/8}.\n\\]\nThe capped nearest distance is bounded below by a fixed multiple of the uncapped nearest distance plus one, because all points lie within diameter \\(As\\).</p><p>Expand by choosing one neighbor \\(j(i)\\ne i\\) for each index. The directed graph has outdegree one; each component is a directed cycle of length at least two with attached in-trees. Set \\(q=1/8\\). Dyadic shells and the counting assumption give\n\\[\n\\sum_{x\\in S}(1+\\|x-y\\|_\\infty)^{-q}\\leq Cs^{\\nu-q},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{x\\in S}(1+\\|x-y\\|_\\infty)^{-2q}\\leq Cs^{\\nu-2q}.\n\\]\nSum in-tree leaves, each costing \\(Cs^{\\nu-q}\\). On the remaining cycle, sum a vertex incident to two cycle edges by Cauchy–Schwarz, costing \\(Cs^{\\nu-2q}\\). For a two-cycle this is the second displayed bound. Sum the resulting path’s leaves at cost \\(Cs^{\\nu-q}\\), and its final vertex at cost \\(Cs^\\nu\\). A component of \\(m\\) vertices costs at most\n\\[\nC_ms^{m(\\nu-q)}.\n\\]\nThere are at most \\((2p-1)^{2p}\\) neighbor choices.</p><p>For large or bent supports, overlay squares of side \\(cs/16\\). At most \\(C(1+A/c)^2\\) meet \\(S\\). In each nonempty piece choose \\(z\\in S\\). The piece lies in \\(z+B_{cs/8}\\), while \\(z+B_{cs/2}\\) is free. Compare that marginal to the plus law in the latter square, apply the estimate, and use the \\(L^{2p}\\) triangle inequality. Bounded-scale rounding is absorbed in constants. The same construction works in periodic charts.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Static correlation inputs — original Lemma 3.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Static correlation inputs — original Lemma 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the critical square-lattice setting and fixed geometric separations of Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The one-arm, connected two-spin, uniqueness, and infinite-plane two-point conclusions in items 1–3 of Proposition 3 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>The centered-square arm estimate is [6, arXiv version 1, Lemma 26]. For another bulk point, take a contained square of radius \\(c'r\\). Its free-boundary arm lower bound remains a lower bound for the arm in the larger domain. Add an open circuit in its outer half-annulus and a rectangle crossing from its inner half to the original boundary. These events join; RSW and association give a fixed-factor lower bound. The upper bound uses a wired arm in a contained square.</p><p>As the boundary recedes, plus magnetization tends to zero. In a monotone coupling of plus and minus laws, disagreement probability at a vertex is half the mean difference. A union bound on each fixed finite set makes the extremal infinite-volume laws equal, proving uniqueness and symmetry.</p><p>For the connected correlation, apply the bulk one- and two-spin convergence of Chelkak–Hongler–Izyurov [4, Theorem 1.3]. In the upper half-plane, put\n\\[\nq=\\frac{|z-z'|}{|z-\\overline{z'}|}\\in(0,1).\n\\]\nThe cited plus one- and two-spin formulas give\n\\[\n\\frac{\\langle\\sigma_z\\sigma_{z'}\\rangle_+}\n{\\langle\\sigma_z\\rangle_+\\langle\\sigma_{z'}\\rangle_+}\n=\n\\left(\\frac{q^{1/2}+q^{-1/2}}2\\right)^{1/2}&gt;1.\n\\]\nConformal covariance transfers strict positivity of the connected function to a rectangle. Continuity and compactness give a positive minimum on separated bulk compact sets. Uniform convergence gives the discrete lower bound for large \\(r\\); finite energy absorbs smaller scales. The upper bound follows from convergence or two disjoint wired arm tests.</p><p>For the plane asymptotic, set \\(R=|x-y|\\) and \\(u=(y-x)/R\\). Bracket the plane two-point function between free and plus laws in a square centered at \\(x\\) of radius \\(TR\\). Fix \\(T&gt;2\\), then take \\(R\\to\\infty\\). The continuum limits \\(F_T^b(0,u)\\), \\(b\\in\\{\\mathrm{free},+\\}\\), satisfy\n\\[\nF_T^b(0,u)=T^{-1/4}F_1^b(0,u/T).\n\\]\nThe normalization and two-spin fusion results cited from [4] give\n\\[\nF_1^b(0,u/T)=T^{1/4}(1+o_T(1))\n\\]\nfor both boundaries, uniformly in direction. Let \\(T\\to\\infty\\) after the fixed-\\(T\\) mesh limit.</p><p>Finally, connecting \\(x,y\\) forces arms in disjoint boxes of radius \\(\\lfloor|x-y|/4\\rfloor\\). Condition on other edges and apply wired arm upper bounds. FK gives nonnegativity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Product arm bound — original Lemma 3.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Product arm bound — original Lemma 3.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Q\\) be a lattice square with an ordinary FK law under arbitrary boundary wiring.</p><p class=\"li\">After sampling bonds, assign boundary-touching actual clusters any jointly distributed signs. Every other cluster has an independent fair sign, independent of all boundary-cluster signs conditional on the bonds.</p><p class=\"li\">The bond law is not further conditioned on compatibility with mixed prescribed signs.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\), \\(n\\geq2\\), lie at distance at least \\(cs\\) from \\(\\partial Q\\), in a set of diameter at most \\(As\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  r_i=\\max\\!\\left(1,\\left\\lfloor\n  \\min\\!\\left(\\frac{cs}{4},\\frac14\\min_{j\\ne i}\\|x_i-x_j\\|_\\infty\\right)\n  \\right\\rfloor\\right).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\left|\\mathbb E\\prod_{i=1}^n\\sigma_{x_i}\\right|\n\\leq C_{n,c}\\prod_{i=1}^nr_i^{-1/8}.\n\\]\nThe same estimate holds conditional on exterior bonds, with induced wiring, before cluster colors are sampled.</p>", "proof": "<p>Cancel insertions occurring an even number of times at a vertex. Original radii at repeated vertices are one, so discarding these factors costs nothing. Keep the original radii of remaining insertions; do not recompute them.</p><p>Each remaining singleton with radius larger than one must have an open arm to its ball boundary if the conditional sign average is nonzero. Otherwise its free cluster contains no other odd insertion and has mean zero. A cluster reaching a prescribed-sign boundary also needs this arm.</p><p>The balls of radius greater than one are disjoint and contained in \\(Q\\). Conditional arm probabilities are bounded by fully wired probabilities. Lemma 3.1 gives \\(Cr_i^{-1/8}\\); sequential conditioning multiplies the bounds. Absolute sign averages are at most one. Radius-one balls and parity cancellations only remove requirements.</p><p>Exterior bond conditioning changes only the boundary wiring, so the same proof applies.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Doubled representation, increasing conditioning, and local domination — original Lemmas 4.3–4.5 and Proposition 4.6.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Doubled representation, increasing conditioning, and local domination — original Lemmas 4.3–4.5 and Proposition 4.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G_{\\mathrm{orig}}=(V,E)\\) be finite, with \\(K_e\\geq0\\) and pin set \\(P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Take two independent Ising configurations \\(\\eta,\\xi\\) whose prescribed signs on \\(P\\) are ordered, with higher signs assigned to \\(\\eta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\subseteq P\\) be disagreement pins and \\(A=P\\setminus U\\) agreement pins, with common signs \\(\\tau\\). Put \\(W=V\\setminus P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\subseteq W\\) be the original replicas’ free disagreement set.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(H\\subseteq V\\setminus P\\), let \\(Z_{P,\\tau}(H)\\) denote the partition sum on the induced graph \\(P\\cup H\\), including edges between pins. In the doubled formulas use couplings \\(J_e=2K_e\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Given \\(G\\), sample FK bonds \\(\\omega\\) on \\(G\\cup U\\). Pad bonds by closed edges off that set.</p><p class=\"li\">Order \\((G,\\omega)\\) by coordinatewise inclusion.</p><p class=\"li\">The plus-agreement reference replaces every sign on \\(A\\) by plus, retaining \\(U\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. The auxiliary set law is\n   \\[\n   \\mu_\\tau(G)\\propto Z_{U,+}(G)\\,Z_{A,\\tau}(W\\setminus G).\n   \\]\n   Conditional on \\(G\\), opposite spins on \\(G\\cup U\\) form the first doubled Ising law, independently of common spins on its complement.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Recoloring yields an ordered coupling of the original marginals with\n   \\[\n   \\eta_x-\\xi_x=2\\mathbf1_{\\{x\\leftrightarrow U\\}},\\qquad\n   \\mathbb E\\eta_x-\\mathbb E\\xi_x=2\\mathbb P(x\\leftrightarrow U).\n   \\]\n   Connections use actual open edges.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The plus-agreement reference law of \\((G,\\omega)\\) is associated. The mixed-agreement law has an increasing density relative to it. Hence, for increasing \\(f\\) and increasing positive-probability \\(\\mathcal E\\),\n   \\[\n   \\mathbb E_\\tau[f\\mid\\mathcal E]\\geq\\mathbb E_+[f].\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Let \\(Q\\subseteq W\\) contain no pins. Construct a box-alone doubled model from two independent original-coupling Ising configurations with plus spins at every exterior neighbor of \\(Q\\); denote its auxiliary law by \\(\\mathbb P_Q^{++}\\). Then\n   \\[\n   \\mathbb E_\\tau[f\\mid\\mathcal E]\\geq\\mathbb E_Q^{++}[f]\n   \\]\n   for every increasing \\(f\\) of the auxiliary set and bonds internal to \\(Q\\). The sure event is allowed.</p><p>No restriction is imposed on the number or placement of pins in this finite-graph statement. Critical FK RSW is not applied to the doubled-coupling bonds.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 4.1 and 4.3 establish the representation, coupling, and increasing-conditioning assertions. It remains to prove local domination, original Proposition 4.6.</p><p>By Lemma 4.3, compare the plus-agreement reference to \\(\\mathbb P_Q^{++}\\). Condition its exterior auxiliary set. Fix \\(v\\in Q\\) and all other interior memberships. Let \\(D\\) be all disagreement vertices excluding \\(v\\), including \\(U\\), and \\(H\\) all agreement vertices excluding \\(v\\), including \\(A\\). The conditional odds are\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mu_+(v\\in G\\mid G\\setminus\\{v\\})}\n{\\mu_+(v\\notin G\\mid G\\setminus\\{v\\})}\n=\n\\frac{g_{U,+,D\\setminus U}(v)}\n{g_{A,+,H\\setminus A}(v)}.\n\\]\nThe numerator is at least the gain on the induced disagreement set in \\(Q\\): adding exterior disagreement vertices, edges, and plus pins increases it.</p><p>For the denominator, pin exterior agreement vertices plus, then restore every missing exterior neighbor of \\(Q\\) as a plus pin. Lemma 4.2 and its nonnegative expansion show that both operations increase the gain. The result is exactly the agreement-site gain of the box-alone model; partition factors involving only pinned exterior vertices cancel.</p><p>Thus the interior-set density relative to the box-alone set law increases under every single-site addition. The box-alone law is associated, so the increasing tilt gives set domination.</p><p>Given the full set, exterior FK bonds induce wiring on its interior part. This dominates the free boundary condition of the box-alone disagreement-bond system, which has no disagreement pins on or outside \\(Q\\). Free FK also increases with the induced vertex set. Compare bonds first, then sets, and average over the exterior set. Combining this with Lemma 4.3 proves the conditional local-domination statement.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Doubled representation and ordered recoloring — original Lemma 4.3.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Doubled representation and ordered recoloring — original Lemma 4.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the finite graph, ordered pin assignments, and auxiliary set \\(G\\) of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The factorization and ordered-coupling identities in items 1–2 of Proposition 4 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>For an edge whose endpoints lie in the same one of \\(G\\cup U\\) and its complement, the replica energies add to twice the energy of their common orientation. For an edge crossing between the sets, they cancel. This proves\n\\[\n\\mu_\\tau(G)\\propto Z_{U,+}(G)Z_{A,\\tau}(W\\setminus G)\n\\]\nand conditional independence. Edges with pinned endpoints contribute constants independent of \\(G\\).</p><p>Each FK cluster in \\(G\\cup U\\) avoiding \\(U\\) has an independent fair orientation. On each such cluster, replace the pair of opposite signs by two copies of the first replica’s sign. The first marginal is unchanged; the second replica’s independent fair cluster signs have been negated, so its conditional marginal is unchanged. Clusters meeting \\(U\\) retain \\((+,-)\\), and agreement sites retain equal signs.</p><p>Thus the coupling is ordered, and its disagreement set is precisely the union of clusters meeting \\(U\\). The auxiliary set \\(G\\) need not remain the actual disagreement set after recoloring.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Spin products and addition of vertices — original Lemma 4.4.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Spin products and addition of vertices — original Lemma 4.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix arbitrary nonnegative couplings \\(J_e\\), pins \\(P\\), retained free vertices \\(H\\subseteq V\\setminus P\\), and partition sums \\(Z_{P,\\tau}(H)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Products below involve vertices already present before any enlargement.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. With all pins plus, spin-product expectations are nonnegative and increase under increasing couplings, adding free vertices and ferromagnetic incident edges, or adding plus pins.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For every finite \\(R\\subseteq P\\cup H\\),\n   \\[\n   \\left|\\mathbb E_{P,\\tau,H}\\prod_{x\\in R}\\sigma_x\\right|\n   \\leq\n   \\mathbb E_{P,+,H}\\prod_{x\\in R}\\sigma_x.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. \\(H\\mapsto Z_{P,+}(H)\\) is log-supermodular.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. For \\(v\\notin P\\cup H\\),\n   \\[\n   \\frac{Z_{P,\\tau}(H\\cup\\{v\\})}{Z_{P,\\tau}(H)}\n   \\leq\n   \\frac{Z_{P,+}(H\\cup\\{v\\})}{Z_{P,+}(H)}.\n   \\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use FK parameters \\(p_e=1-e^{-2J_e}\\). An actual cluster is an open-edge component; virtual wires do not count as paths. Let \\(k_0(\\omega)\\) count actual clusters avoiding \\(P\\). Up to a constant, the all-plus bond weight is\n\\[\n\\prod_{e\\in E(P\\cup H)}\np_e^{\\omega_e}(1-p_e)^{1-\\omega_e}\\,2^{k_0(\\omega)}.\n\\]\nThis is FK with pins wired.</p><p>Let \\(F_R\\) be the event that every actual cluster avoiding \\(P\\) contains an even number of vertices of \\(R\\). Cluster-sign averaging gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{P,+,H}\\prod_{x\\in R}\\sigma_x=\\phi_{P,+,H}(F_R).\n\\]\nThe event is increasing: merging even-parity unpinned clusters preserves parity, and joining a pinned cluster removes the restriction. Adding a vertex outside \\(R\\) adds parity zero. FK monotonicity proves item 1, including additional plus pinning. Positive fields, when needed, may be represented by edges to a plus vertex.</p><p>For mixed signs, set\n\\[\nD_\\tau=\\{\\text{no actual cluster contains opposite-sign pins}\\}.\n\\]\nThe mixed bond law is the all-plus law conditioned on the decreasing positive-probability event \\(D_\\tau\\). Conditional sign averaging has absolute value at most \\(\\mathbf1_{F_R}\\). Association therefore gives\n\\[\n\\left|\\mathbb E_{P,\\tau,H}\\prod_{x\\in R}\\sigma_x\\right|\n\\leq\\phi(F_R\\mid D_\\tau)\\leq\\phi(F_R).\n\\]\nThis includes edges between opposite pins, which must be closed.</p><p>Adding one free vertex gives the exact site gain\n\\[\ng_{P,\\tau,H}(v)\n=\\frac{Z_{P,\\tau}(H\\cup\\{v\\})}{Z_{P,\\tau}(H)}\n=2\\mathbb E_{P,\\tau,H}\n\\cosh\\!\\left(\\sum_{u\\in P\\cup H}J_{vu}\\sigma_u\\right).\n\\]\nFor its neighbor set \\(N\\),\n\\[\n\\cosh\\!\\left(\\sum_{u\\in N}J_{vu}\\sigma_u\\right)\n=\n\\prod_{u\\in N}\\cosh J_{vu}\n\\sum_{\\substack{R\\subseteq N\\\\|R|\\text{ even}}}\n\\left(\\prod_{u\\in R}\\tanh J_{vu}\\right)\n\\prod_{u\\in R}\\sigma_u.\n\\]\nAll coefficients are nonnegative. Under plus pins, enlarging \\(H\\) increases old product expectations, adds nonnegative terms, and increases the prefactor. Thus site gains increase with \\(H\\). Comparing gains while adding \\(H_1\\setminus H_2\\) proves\n\\[\nZ_{P,+}(H_1\\cup H_2)Z_{P,+}(H_1\\cap H_2)\n\\geq Z_{P,+}(H_1)Z_{P,+}(H_2).\n\\]\nApplying item 2 termwise to the expansion proves the mixed-versus-plus site-gain inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Association and increasing conditioning — original Lemma 4.5.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Association and increasing conditioning — original Lemma 4.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 4. The density of the mixed-agreement set law relative to the plus-agreement set law is, up to normalization,\n\\[\nr(G)=\\frac{Z_{A,\\tau}(W\\setminus G)}\n{Z_{A,+}(W\\setminus G)}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The plus-agreement joint set-and-bond law is associated, \\(r\\) is increasing, and for increasing \\(f\\) and increasing positive-probability \\(\\mathcal E\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\tau[f\\mid\\mathcal E]\\geq\\mathbb E_+[f].\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma 4.2, both plus-pinned factors in the auxiliary set weight are log-supermodular in \\(G\\); complementation exchanges unions and intersections and preserves this property. Their product is log-supermodular, hence associated.</p><p>Given \\(G\\), the FK law is associated and increases stochastically with \\(G\\), by adding isolated vertices and then their edges. Conditional expectations of increasing functions of \\((G,\\omega)\\) increase with \\(G\\). Decomposing covariance into conditional covariance and covariance of conditional expectations proves joint association.</p><p>The site-gain inequality of Lemma 4.2 says that\n\\[\n\\frac{Z_{A,\\tau}(H)}{Z_{A,+}(H)}\n\\]\ndecreases when \\(H\\) grows. Therefore \\(r(G)\\) increases. The mixed law conditioned on \\(\\mathcal E\\) has density proportional to \\(r\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal E}\\), an increasing function, against the associated reference. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathbb E_+[fr\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal E}]}\n{\\mathbb E_+[r\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal E}]}\n\\geq\\mathbb E_+[f].\n\\]\nNo association of the mixed law, or preservation of a lattice condition after event conditioning, is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Mass at a source-connected patch — original Proposition 4.9.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Mass at a source-connected patch — original Proposition 4.9.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in a square-lattice chart at \\(\\beta_c\\), using the doubled auxiliary model of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\) be the disagreement pins; all common pins may have arbitrary signs.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(a&gt;0\\), \\(b_0\\subseteq z+B_w\\), and a horizontal or vertical segment \\(L\\subseteq z+B_w\\) with\n  \\[\n  aw\\leq|L|\\leq2w.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(z+B_{16w}\\) is a pin-free planar lattice chart.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large \\(w\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{x\\in L}\\mathbb P(x\\leftrightarrow U)\n\\geq c_aw^{7/8}\\mathbb P(U\\leftrightarrow b_0).\n\\]\nThe constant and threshold depend only on \\(a\\). The same statement holds in an isometric planar chart of a torus or cylinder.</p>", "proof": "<p>Translate \\(z\\) to zero. Let \\(L'\\) be the translate of \\(L\\) by \\(6w\\) in a perpendicular direction. Both lie in fixed bulk subsets of \\(B_{16w}\\), and \\(L'\\) is outside \\(B_{4w}\\).</p><p>Lemma 5.1 gives positive probability that at least \\(cw^{7/8}\\) sites of \\(L\\) connect to \\(L'\\) by internal bonds. Intersect with the increasing circuit event of Lemma 5.2. Association bounds their joint probability below by \\(p&gt;0\\).</p><p>If \\(\\mathbb P(U\\leftrightarrow b_0)=0\\), there is nothing to prove. Otherwise condition on this increasing event. Proposition 4 preserves the lower bound for the joint local event.</p><p>Every path from \\(U\\), outside \\(B_{16w}\\), to \\(b_0\\subseteq B_w\\) meets the surrounding circuit. Every counted path from \\(L\\) to \\(L'\\) also meets it. Planarity joins all counted sites to \\(U\\). Their conditional expected number is at least \\(pcw^{7/8}\\). Multiply by \\(\\mathbb P(U\\leftrightarrow b_0)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Many sites joined across a rectangle — original Lemma 4.7.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Many sites joined across a rectangle — original Lemma 4.7.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Take two independent plus-boundary critical Ising configurations in a rectangle \\(Q\\) of scale \\(w\\) and fixed aspect ratio.</p><p class=\"li\">Use their auxiliary disagreement bonds before recoloring.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L_1,L_2\\) be straight segments of lengths comparable to \\(w\\), in fixed compact bulk subsets, separated by distance comparable to \\(w\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large \\(w\\), there are geometry-dependent \\(c,p&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_Q^{++}\n\\left(\n|\\{x\\in L_1:x\\leftrightarrow L_2\\}|\\geq cw^{7/8}\n\\right)\\geq p.\n\\]\nIn particular, the segment-connection probability is bounded below.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the original independent replicas set\n\\[\nY_x=(\\eta_x-\\xi_x)/2.\n\\]\nThere are no disagreement pins. Conditional cluster signs give\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_Q^{++}(x\\leftrightarrow y)\n=\\mathbb E[Y_xY_y]\n=\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Cov}_Q^+(\\sigma_x,\\sigma_y).\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_Q^{++}(x\\leftrightarrow y,\\ x'\\leftrightarrow y')\n\\leq\\mathbb E[Y_xY_yY_{x'}Y_{y'}].\n\\]\nIf both pairs connect, every auxiliary cluster has even parity among the four indexed factors. Conditional averaging of their product is the indicator of that even-parity event, with all factors required to belong to the auxiliary set. This also covers repeated indices.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nX=\\sum_{x\\in L_1}\\sum_{y\\in L_2}\\mathbf1_{\\{x\\leftrightarrow y\\}}.\n\\]\nProposition 3 gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb EX\\geq c_1w^{7/4}.\n\\]\nThe four-point inequality, Cauchy–Schwarz, and fourth line moments give\n\\[\n\\mathbb EX^2\n\\leq\n\\mathbb E\\left[\n\\left(\\sum_{L_1}Y_x\\right)^2\n\\left(\\sum_{L_2}Y_y\\right)^2\n\\right]\n\\leq C_1w^{7/2}.\n\\]\nMoments for \\(Y\\) follow from replica moments by the \\(L^4\\) triangle inequality. Paley–Zygmund gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(X\\geq c_2w^{7/4})\\geq p_2.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(N\\) be the largest number of \\(L_2\\)-sites in one auxiliary cluster. Then\n\\[\nN^2\\leq\\sum_C|C\\cap L_2|^2,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E\\sum_C|C\\cap L_2|^2\n=\n\\mathbb E\\left(\\sum_{L_2}Y_x\\right)^2\n\\leq C_2w^{7/4}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(K\\) with \\(C_2/K^2&lt;p_2/2\\). With probability at least \\(p_2/2\\), both\n\\[\nX\\geq c_2w^{7/4},\\qquad N\\leq Kw^{7/8}\n\\]\nhold. Each site of \\(L_1\\) reaching \\(L_2\\) contributes at most \\(N\\) to \\(X\\), giving at least \\((c_2/K)w^{7/8}\\) such sites.</p><p>The event in the statement is increasing, although the auxiliary bound involving \\(N\\) was not used as an increasing event.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**A surrounding disagreement circuit — original Lemma 4.8.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**A surrounding disagreement circuit — original Lemma 4.8.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the doubled plus-boundary auxiliary law on \\(B_{16w}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large \\(w\\), a circuit of actual disagreement bonds in\n\\[\nB_{4w}\\setminus B_{3w/2}\n\\]\nsurrounding \\(B_{3w/2}\\) has probability at least \\(p_0&gt;0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the four strips\n\\[\n[-4w,4w]\\times[2w,4w],\\quad\n[-4w,4w]\\times[-4w,-2w],\n\\]\n\\[\n[2w,4w]\\times[-4w,4w],\\quad\n[-4w,-2w]\\times[-4w,4w].\n\\]\nFor the top strip extend the long coordinate to \\([-5w,5w]\\), with agreeing plus boundary. Apply Lemma 5.1 to vertical segments at \\(x=\\pm4w\\), with \\(y\\in[5w/2,7w/2]\\). Their connection stays inside the extended rectangle because agreeing boundary pins cannot belong to the auxiliary disagreement set. A subpath crosses the unextended top strip.</p><p>Proposition 4 transfers the lower bound to the doubled law on \\(B_{16w}\\). Repeat for the other strips. Association gives a positive lower bound for all four increasing crossings simultaneously.</p><p>Adjacent crossings intersect in their corner overlap, where they cross perpendicular coordinates. Join successive intersections, erase portions beyond adjacent overlaps, and obtain a closed walk of winding number one about the origin in the annulus. Decomposing it into simple circuits leaves one with nonzero winding number.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Ordered transmission and its random, signed, and domain forms — original Proposition 4.2 and Corollaries 4.11–4.12.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Ordered transmission and its random, signed, and domain forms — original Proposition 4.2 and Corollaries 4.11–4.12.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in critical square-lattice charts, with no external fields except prescribed-spin fields.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S,T\\) be disjoint source and target sets. Common pins are arbitrary.</p><p class=\"li\">After deleting common pinned vertices, a finite set \\(J\\), disjoint from pins, source, and target, is a separating cut if every path from \\(S\\) to \\(T\\) visits \\(J\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(A,K\\geq1\\), \\(a\\in(0,1/(16A))\\), and \\(0&lt;w\\leq s\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The cut is admissible at scales \\(w,s\\), as in original Definition 4.1, if it has a partition into patches \\(b_i\\), counting segments \\(L_i\\subseteq J\\), centers \\(z_i\\), and scales \\(\\ell_i\\in[aw,Aw]\\), such that:\n  \\[\n  b_i\\cup L_i\\subseteq z_i+B_{\\ell_i},\\qquad\n  a\\ell_i\\leq|L_i|\\leq2\\ell_i;\n  \\]\n  each \\(z_i+B_{16\\ell_i}\\) is a pin-free planar chart, including freedom from source pins; every site of \\(J\\) belongs to at most \\(K\\) counting segments; and\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{dist}(z_i,T)\\geq s/A,\\qquad16\\ell_i\\leq s/A,\n  \\]\n  with the small-patch comparison applicable up to a fixed positive multiple of \\(s\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  M_J=s^{-7/8}\\sum_{x\\in J}\\sigma_x.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Decrease the comparison exponent, if needed, to \\(\\alpha\\in(0,1/8)\\). This is the exponent denoted \\(\\delta\\) in original Section 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. For two ordered prescribed source configurations and identical common pins, let \\(\\Delta\\) mean higher-law minus lower-law expectation. For sufficiently large \\(w\\),\n   \\[\n   |\\Delta h|\n   \\leq C\\|h\\|_\\infty(w/s)^{-7/8+\\alpha}\\Delta M_J\n   \\]\n   for every bounded target observable \\(h\\). Equivalently, for fixed \\(\\|h\\|_\\infty\\leq H\\),\n   \\[\n   \\Delta M_J\\geq c_H(w/s)^{7/8-\\alpha}|\\Delta h|.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The same holds after averaging over an ordered coupling of random source assignments with their common Gibbs continuation.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(\\mu\\) is such a Gibbs law and \\(q=q(\\sigma_S)\\) satisfies\n   \\[\n   \\mathbb E_\\mu q=0,\\qquad\n   \\mathbb E_\\mu[qf]\\geq0\n   \\quad\\text{for every increasing source function }f,\n   \\]\n   then\n   \\[\n   |\\mathbb E_\\mu[qh]|\n   \\leq C\\|h\\|_\\infty(w/s)^{-7/8+\\alpha}\n   \\mathbb E_\\mu[qM_J].\n   \\]\n   Such \\(q\\) is an order-positive centered signed density, as in original Definition 4.10; it need not be nonnegative or increasing. Constants do not depend on its positive mass.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. If two ordered laws, possibly on different volumes or pin sets, have a common free source contour \\(S_0\\) and identical conditional continuation from \\(S_0\\) toward \\(T\\), with an admissible cut \\(J\\) between them, then\n   \\[\n   \\|\\mu_T^+-\\mu_T^-\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n   \\leq C(w/s)^{-7/8+\\alpha}\n   \\bigl(\\mathbb E_{\\mu^+}M_J-\\mathbb E_{\\mu^-}M_J\\bigr).\n   \\]\n   All differing pins must be on the source side of \\(S_0\\).</p><p>Constants depend only on the admissibility parameters and fixed chart geometry, not on the number or signs of source pins or on the mesh. They remain uniform as \\(w/s\\) decreases within this geometry.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 6.1 proves the deterministic assertion and its rearranged form.</p><p>For an ordered random pair of sources, integrate the deterministic bound. Magnetic differences are nonnegative; the triangle inequality bounds the absolute integrated target difference.</p><p>For the signed assertion, let\n\\[\nm=\\mathbb E_\\mu q_+=\\mathbb E_\\mu q_-.\n\\]\nIf \\(m=0\\), the claim is immediate. Otherwise define source probability laws\n\\[\n\\mu_\\pm=m^{-1}q_\\pm\\mu_S.\n\\]\nOrder positivity gives \\(\\mu_+(F)\\geq\\mu_-(F)\\) for every increasing set \\(F\\).</p><p>These laws admit an ordered coupling. Indeed, transport mass from each lower configuration \\(x\\) to upper configurations \\(y\\geq x\\). For any set \\(B\\) of lower configurations, the required max-flow/min-cut inequality is\n\\[\n\\mu_-(B)\\leq\\mu_-(\\uparrow B)\\leq\\mu_+(\\uparrow B).\n\\]\nApply the random-source bound and multiply by \\(m\\). Common Gibbs continuation identifies the resulting differences with \\(\\mathbb E_\\mu[qh]\\) and \\(\\mathbb E_\\mu[qM_J]\\).</p><p>For ordered domain or pin changes, restrict an ordered coupling to \\(S_0\\) and use the common continuation toward \\(T\\). Apply the random-source form. Taking \\(h\\) to be each target-event indicator gives the total-variation inequality.</p><p>Concentric square contours and parallel periodic rows in common free collars satisfy admissibility by partitioning into sufficiently short intervals and choosing nearby straight counting segments, with bounded reuse near corners. These choices remain uniform as \\(w/s\\) decreases. As recorded in original Remark 5.3, for \\(N\\) cuts with \\(w/s\\asymp N^{-1}\\), constants are independent of \\(N\\); choose \\(N\\) first, then a sufficiently fine mesh.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Deterministic-source transmission — original Proposition 4.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Deterministic-source transmission — original Proposition 4.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the admissible cut, normalization, and two ordered prescribed source configurations of Proposition 6.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n|\\Delta h|\n\\leq C\\|h\\|_\\infty(w/s)^{-7/8+\\alpha}\\Delta M_J.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the ordered coupling of Proposition 4, and write its cut configurations as \\(\\eta_J\\geq\\xi_J\\). The separating property gives a common continuation\n\\[\nk(j)=\\mathbb E[h\\mid\\sigma_J=j],\n\\]\nindependent of the source assignment. Hence\n\\[\n\\Delta h=\\mathbb E[k(\\eta_J)-k(\\xi_J)].\n\\]</p><p>Interpolate one patch at a time. Other cut spins are common pins for each comparison. Proposition 2 bounds one patch’s oscillation by\n\\[\nC\\|h\\|_\\infty(w/s)^\\alpha.\n\\]\nA summand vanishes if the sampled configurations agree on its patch. By Proposition 4, disagreement in \\(b_i\\) occurs exactly when \\(U\\leftrightarrow b_i\\), where \\(U\\) is the disagreement pin set. Therefore\n\\[\n|\\Delta h|\n\\leq C\\|h\\|_\\infty(w/s)^\\alpha\n\\sum_i\\mathbb P(U\\leftrightarrow b_i).\n\\]\nApply Proposition 5 at scale \\(\\ell_i\\asymp w\\), in the original coupling whose counting charts remain free. Bounded overlap gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_i\\mathbb P(U\\leftrightarrow b_i)\n&amp;\\leq Cw^{-7/8}\\sum_i\\sum_{x\\in L_i}\n\\mathbb P(U\\leftrightarrow x)\\\\\n&amp;\\leq CKw^{-7/8}\\sum_{x\\in J}\\mathbb P(U\\leftrightarrow x).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last sum equals \\(\\tfrac12s^{7/8}\\Delta M_J\\). Substitute to obtain the estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Convergence of every complete interface resolution — original Lemma 6.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Convergence of every complete interface resolution — original Lemma 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Consider a plus Jordan domain whose lattice boundary converges uniformly modulo parametrization.</p><p class=\"li\">An oriented Ising circuit is a closed dual path without repeated edges, with plus spins on its left and minus spins on its right; it may visit a dual vertex twice.</p><p class=\"li\">At each checkerboard face, choose either noncrossing pairing of incoming and outgoing disagreement edges.</p><p class=\"li\">A complete resolution uses every disagreement edge exactly once.</p><p class=\"li\">The canonical leftmost collection uses the strong-path convention: adjacent plus spins on the left are successively nearest-neighbor connected.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d_u\\) denote uniform distance modulo orientation-preserving parametrization, also allowing a changed starting point for loops.</p><p class=\"li\">Compare collections by matching loops and charging diameters of unmatched loops, as in the cited Benoist–Hongler collection metric.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every complete resolution converges to the same oriented nested \\(\\mathrm{CLE}_3\\) collection, uniformly over the resolution choice. This includes the zero-set resolution obtained by fixing one diagonal in every square face. Orientations and nesting colors at positive scales converge as well.</p>", "proof": "<p>Benoist–Hongler [2, arXiv version 3, Theorem 6] gives leftmost-loop convergence and the simultaneous assertion that, for each \\(e&gt;0\\), with probability tending to one every circuit \\(\\ell\\) of diameter greater than \\(e\\) has a leftmost circuit \\(\\ell_L\\) with\n\\[\nd_u(\\ell,\\ell_L)\\leq e,\n\\]\nand every component of\n\\[\n(\\ell\\cup\\ell_L)\\setminus(\\ell\\cap\\ell_L)\n\\]\nhas diameter less than \\(e\\). The cited paragraph after that theorem gives the common-edge consequence: shared edges become dense in each macroscopic loop. The event applies to all circuits, including configuration-dependent selections.</p><p>From any vanishing-mesh sequence, choose \\(e\\downarrow0\\) slowly, pass to a subsequence, and couple leftmost collections to their limit so both collection error and all-circuits error vanish almost surely. Choose a diameter cutoff not equal to any limit-loop diameter. There are finitely many limit loops above it; they are simple, disjoint, and have positive pairwise distances.</p><p>Any two complete resolutions are joined by changing checkerboard pairings one at a time. Each switch merges two edge-simple circuits or splits one into two. All intermediate circuits remain in the class covered by the simultaneous assertion.</p><p>Suppose selected switches change the number of circuits matched near a fixed macroscopic limit loop. Pass to a subsequence so each constituent either has vanishing diameter or converges parametrically to a simple limit loop. Two vanishing constituents cannot merge macroscopically because they meet at the switched vertex. Adding a vanishing constituent does not change a large constituent’s limit. If both are large, their meeting forces their limit loops to meet, hence to be the same simple loop.</p><p>Choose a point strictly inside that loop. Parametrized convergence preserves winding number. Each constituent has winding number \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\); the merged traversal has their sum, \\(0\\) or \\(\\pm2\\). Yet the merged circuit is also covered by the all-circuits assertion and approaches the same oriented simple loop, so its winding number must be \\(\\pm1\\), a contradiction. Splits are reversed merges. Selecting an offending switch avoids accumulating errors over all switches.</p><p>A specified leftmost circuit can be included in a complete resolution by fixing its visited pairings and resolving unused edges. At a once-visited checkerboard vertex the unused pair is the remaining noncrossing pair; at a twice-visited vertex both pairs are already specified.</p><p>In that resolution, another circuit matched to the same limit loop would, by uniqueness of the nearby macroscopic leftmost loop and the common-edge consequence, share an edge with the specified circuit. This contradicts single use of edges. Thus the count is one in that resolution, hence in every resolution. The all-circuits assertion excludes unmatched macroscopic circuits.</p><p>Colors follow from exterior plus and nesting. Every ancestor of a loop of diameter at least \\(a&gt;0\\) also has diameter at least \\(a\\); there are finitely many. Their nesting and orientations are stable under convergence to disjoint simple loops.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Stopping transcripts and convergence of signed facing data — original Lemmas 7.2 and 7.4, Definition 7.3, and Remark 7.5.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Stopping transcripts and convergence of signed facing data — original Lemmas 7.2 and 7.4, Definition 7.3, and Remark 7.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use an inward or outward band with the fixed positive clearances described in Theorem 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Initial contour vertices are revealed. Band spins include vertices of triangles meeting the closed rounded band.</p><p class=\"li\">The complete transcript records the adaptive triangle-spin reveal.</p><p class=\"li\">To record data on exact requested contours, choose disjoint fixed intervals around the two permitted radius ranges. Let \\(f_\\delta\\) be increasing and piecewise linear, send \\(u_\\delta\\) to \\(u\\), \\(v_\\delta\\) to \\(v\\), fix interval endpoints, and equal the identity outside. Set\n  \\[\n  R_\\delta(0)=0,\\qquad\n  R_\\delta(z)=\\frac{f_\\delta(\\|z\\|_\\infty)}{\\|z\\|_\\infty}z\n  \\quad(z\\neq0).\n  \\]\n  Both \\(R_\\delta\\) and its inverse move points by \\(O(\\delta)\\), uniformly in permitted radii. Physical spins and Gibbs laws are not moved.</p><p class=\"li\">A returning chord is a maximal loop portion on the fresh side returning to the starting contour before reaching the second contour. A stopped strand is retained only up to its first hit of the second contour.</p><p class=\"li\">Cut along these portions, retain the component containing the untraced region beyond the second contour, and keep only boundary pieces visible from it.</p><p class=\"li\">An admissible facing datum consists of an oriented Jordan base and finitely many disjoint simple attached slits at distinct roots, with marked roots and tips and signed boundary arcs. Slit-side signs are opposite. Prime-boundary traversal goes down one side to the tip and back along the other: signs switch at the tip and continue unchanged through each root occurrence. Tips lie on the second contour; the datum lies in the closed band. Without slits, the base has a constant side sign.</p><p class=\"li\">Lattice data are recorded using \\(R_\\delta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X_{\\mathrm{band}}\\) use the matching metric described in Theorem 5, and let \\(\\widehat X\\) be the ambient marked-walk space there. When radii vary, \\(X_{\\mathrm{band}}\\) is the union over permitted choices; numerical radii are not additional marks.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. At each mesh, conditional on a complete transcript, unexamined spins have the Gibbs law with exactly the examined spins fixed.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The reveal lies on the starting side and in an \\(O(\\delta)\\) enlargement of the band. Traces, signs, and facing data are functions of band spins alone.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For almost every deterministic pair of contour radii, nested \\(\\mathrm{CLE}_3\\) yields an admissible facing datum, possibly with infinitely many returning chords in its Jordan base but only finitely many slits.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Lattice facing data converge in \\(X_{\\mathrm{band}}\\), jointly for any fixed finite list of bands.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. The ambient space and admissible-data space are separable.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 8.1 gives the stopping and measurability assertions. For generic deterministic levels, Fubini applied to the countable exceptional-level sets in Lemma 8.2 yields a deterministic full-measure set of radius choices.</p><p>The only remaining color issue is a datum without tips. Use the nested-\\(\\mathrm{CLE}_3\\) property cited as [14, Section 1 before Proposition 1]: a deterministic interior point is almost surely surrounded by infinitely many nested loops. By local finiteness their diameters tend to zero.</p><p>Choose such a deterministic point on the starting contour. A sufficiently small surrounding loop misses the second contour and has a returning excursion into the fresh side. If this chord is hidden, a screening chord has diameter bounded away from zero by the small-pocket argument. There are only finitely many such screening chords, so one is visible. Its oriented parent loop supplies the constant side sign. That loop and its finitely many ancestors converge with orientation by Proposition 7.</p><p>Apply Lemma 8.2, Proposition 7, and the continuous mapping theorem, jointly for finitely many bands.</p><p>For separability, approximate finitely many parametrized walks by rational polygonal walks, approximating shared endpoints and roots together. Prototypes need not be simple or satisfy exact band constraints. They are countable and dense within each finite combinatorial type. The countable union over types is separable. A countable ambient base restricts to one on \\(X_{\\mathrm{band}}\\).</p><p>No universal lower bound on band width is required: all widths and clearances are positive and fixed before the mesh limit, as in original Remark 7.1. When radii vary, reserve a larger allowed window and choose common source and test contours clear of its full range. Regular barriers may use room in that reserved window rather than lie between each realization’s numerical radii.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Adaptive stopping property — original Lemma 7.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Adaptive stopping property — original Lemma 7.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the fixed triangulation, deterministic endpoint-exploration order, rounded contours, and full transcript of Proposition 8.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The reveal is finite and adaptive. Conditioning on a transcript fixes precisely its recorded spins and leaves the original Ising interaction weights on all other variables. The reveal’s location and band measurability are as stated in items 1–2 of Proposition 8.</p>", "proof": "<p>There are finitely many starting edges and triangles. At each step, the previously exposed zero segment and diagonal convention determine the next triangle; its spins determine continuation. The event that a specified transcript occurs depends only on its recorded spins. In the finite Gibbs density, conditioning on that event therefore fixes those spins and leaves the original interaction weights on remaining variables, including when the examined set is random.</p><p>The trace stops at the second contour and examines adjacent triangles only. Although the initial reveal includes an entire side of the band, computing a strand inside the band uses only adjacent starting-contour spins and band spins. The visible datum uses these traces and their local signs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Generic-level geometry and continuity — the deterministic argument in original Lemma 7.4.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Generic-level geometry and continuity — the deterministic argument in original Lemma 7.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let a loop collection consist of simple disjoint loops and be locally finite above every positive diameter.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose contour levels that are not local maximum or minimum values of the square-radius function along any loop, and contain no constant loop interval.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the visible-boundary and facing-data construction of Proposition 8.</p><p class=\"li\">For convergence, assume complete loop collections converge uniformly modulo parametrization with the coverage, multiplicity, orientation, and nesting properties of Proposition 7.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The visible boundary has a Jordan base with finitely many disjoint slits. The base, marked slits, and, when slits are present, side signs converge in the stated matching metric.</p>", "proof": "<p>A continuous real function on a compact parameter interval has countably many local maximum and minimum values: each is an extremum on an interval with rational endpoints inside a witnessing neighborhood. There are countably many loops, so each collection has only countably many exceptional levels.</p><p>Only finitely many excursions have diameter greater than a fixed \\(a&gt;0\\). Only finitely many parent loops have such diameter, and uniform continuity gives a positive minimum parameter length for each such excursion on each loop. Excursion parameter intervals are disjoint. Every stopped strand spans the fixed contour separation, hence there are finitely many.</p><p>Tips are distinct: coincident stopped ends of one excursion would be a one-sided second-level contact. Excursion endpoints on the starting contour are distinct by disjointness of different loops, simplicity of each loop, and exclusion of one-sided contacts.</p><p>Each visible returning chord deletes the open starting-contour interval cut off from the target. Visible deletion intervals have disjoint interiors; in nested deletions the inner chord is screened.</p><p>A small chord deletes a small interval. Join its endpoints by the short starting-square arc. As chord diameter tends to zero, inverse-parametrization continuity makes that arc small. The resulting small pocket cannot contain the target beyond the positively separated second contour, so the erased interval is the short arc, not its long complement. The same argument works on the exterior side, or after a fixed inversion away from the band.</p><p>Parametrize the starting contour and replace each deleted interval by its chord with matching endpoints. At accumulations, interval and chord diameters tend to zero, so the resulting map is continuous. It is injective because chord interiors miss the starting contour and one another, endpoints are distinct, and retained contour points remain distinct. Thus the base is Jordan, even with infinitely many small chords. Add the finitely many visible stopped strands.</p><p>For continuity, choose \\(a&gt;0\\) outside the countable excursion-diameter set and retain excursions larger than \\(a\\). Uniform convergence gives their endpoints and first hits. Compact subarcs strictly on one side of a level remain there. Arbitrarily close to an endpoint on the other side in parameter time, the limiting loop has points on the opposite side of the level; otherwise there is a one-sided contact. These points bracket perturbed endpoints and first hits. Splitting, merging, or losing a retained excursion contradicts this bracketing.</p><p>The retained endpoint order and visibility are stable. A small chord cannot screen a retained chord because its pocket is small. Match retained pieces, then remaining base intervals in circular order; their discrepancies vanish as \\(a\\downarrow0\\). The fixed band width and generic second level give eventual equality of the finite stopped-strand list and its root and tip order. Only returning chords require the final \\(a\\downarrow0\\) limit. The recording map adds \\(O(\\delta)\\) and enforces exact contour incidences.</p><p>At lattice level, replacing a returning interval preserves its surviving side sign. Only stopped tips change signs in prime-boundary traversal. Thus, with slits present, oriented slit sides determine intervening base signs, and Proposition 7 gives sign convergence.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Regular outlines and marked convergence — original Lemmas 8.2–8.4.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Regular outlines and marked convergence — original Lemmas 8.2–8.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let a facing datum have a Jordan base with finitely many disjoint attached simple slits at distinct roots.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D\\) be its target-side disk with slits, or its target-side annulus with slits attached to the inner boundary.</p><p class=\"li\">In the annular case, the other boundary is a fixed disjoint Jordan wall with a fixed finite collection of nondegenerate signed arcs.</p><p class=\"li\">A fixed compact target is disjoint from the slit graph.</p><p class=\"li\">Use a topological chart straightening the base and slits as in Lemma 9.1. Widths and displacements below are measured in this chart; no Lipschitz regularity is assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">Replace each slit by a ribbon whose width and displacement tend to zero, and offset the base by a quantity tending to zero. Use straight strips away from nodes and fixed scaled polygonal templates at nodes, with no neck narrower than a fixed fraction of template scale.</p><p class=\"li\">Regularize the outlines by ambient homeomorphisms of vanishing physical displacement.</p><p class=\"li\">Preserve boundary-component labels, side-sign order, one cap switch approaching each designated tip, and any additional finitely many base switches away from roots.</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize disk uniformizers by \\(F(0)=z_*\\), \\(F'(0)&gt;0\\); normalize annular inverse maps by \\(g(z_*)&gt;0\\), preserving boundary labels.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The resulting regular domains \\(D_q\\) converge to \\(D\\) in marked Carathéodory topology. Their normalized forward uniformizers converge uniformly up to boundary, with radial identification of source annuli when needed. Cap marks converge to the prime ends at their designated tips.</p><p>For continuum critical Ising one-point functions \\(m_q,m\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_{z\\in K}|m_q(z)-m(z)|\\longrightarrow0\n\\qquad(K\\Subset D).\n\\]\nThe arc assumptions concern fixed finitely many nondegenerate arcs; arbitrary mesh-dependent wall assignments are treated separately by Proposition 11.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 9.1–9.2 give the permitted charts and regularizations. Prove convergence along any null sequence of outline parameters.</p><p>In the straightened chart, each filled complementary component is a base region with finitely many thin rectangular teeth. Nearby points in a tooth or base can be joined by a segment or two-segment path of diameter at most a fixed multiple of their distance. The finitely many scaled node templates have scale-invariant finite local quasiconvexity constants. Nonincident pieces have positive separation. Thus, uniformly in the sequence, nearby points of one filled complementary component can be joined there by an arc of diameter at most \\(Ct\\).</p><p>Let \\(H\\) be the fixed chart map and \\(J_q\\) the regularization map. Since \\(J_q,J_q^{-1}\\to\\mathrm{id}\\) uniformly, \\(H_q=J_q\\circ H\\) and its inverse have common moduli of continuity \\(\\mu,\\nu\\). Hence physical complementary components have the uniform joining modulus\n\\[\n\\theta(t)=\\mu(C\\nu(t))\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThe fixed other annular component has such a modulus by its Jordan parametrization. This concerns filled components, not their boundary curves.</p><p>The labeled complementary components converge in Hausdorff distance, and compact subsets of \\(D\\) are eventually in \\(D_q\\). The disk kernel theorem gives interior uniformizer convergence.</p><p>For annuli, write source annuli as \\(A(r_q,1)\\). A fixed smooth essential annulus compactly contained in \\(D\\) lies eventually in every \\(D_q\\); every \\(D_q\\) lies in a fixed bounded essential annulus whose inner disk is in the inner complement. Modulus monotonicity gives\n\\[\n0&lt;r_-\\leq r_q\\leq r_+&lt;1.\n\\]\nAlong a subsequence let \\(r_q\\to r\\). Take simultaneous normal limits of inverse maps \\(g_q\\) on \\(D\\) and forward maps \\(F_q\\) on \\(A(r,1)\\).</p><p>The inverse maps are bounded away from zero and have winding number one on a fixed essential curve. Their limit is not constant, since a nonzero constant has winding number zero. The maximum and minimum principles give \\(r&lt;|g|&lt;1\\). Passing to the identity \\(F_q(g_q(z))=z\\) gives \\(F(g(z))=z\\).</p><p>For every finite \\(a\\notin D\\), Hausdorff convergence provides \\(a_q\\notin D_q\\) with \\(a_q\\to a\\). The zero-free functions \\(F_q-a_q\\), Hurwitz’s theorem, and nonconstancy imply \\(F(w)\\neq a\\). Therefore \\(F(A(r,1))\\subseteq D\\). Pass to \\(g_q(F_q(w))=w\\), obtaining conformal inverses. Uniqueness of modulus and normalization identifies every subsequential limit.</p><p>For boundary equicontinuity, Jordan boundary extension applies to each regular target. Their bounded images and univalence give\n\\[\n\\int|F_q'(z)|^2\\,dA(z)\\leq A_0.\n\\]\nNear a source boundary point \\(\\zeta\\), choose \\(R\\) uniformly below source collar width and normalizing-point boundary distance. For circular source crosscuts \\(C_t\\), \\(r&lt;t&lt;R\\), with image length \\(\\ell_q(t)\\), Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\int_r^R\\frac{\\ell_q(t)^2}{t}\\,dt\\leq2\\pi A_0.\n\\]\nFor some \\(t\\in(r,R)\\),\n\\[\n\\ell_q(t)\\leq s(r)\n=\\left(\\frac{2\\pi A_0}{\\log(R/r)}\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nJoin image endpoints in their common filled complementary component by a simple arc of diameter at most \\(\\theta(s(r))\\). Together with the image crosscut this forms a small Jordan curve. For small \\(r\\), its containing ball excludes \\(z_*\\), and in the annular case also cannot meet the other complementary component.</p><p>The source cap does not contain the source normalizing point, so its image is on the small bounded side. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{diam}F_q(B(\\zeta,r)\\cap\\text{source domain})\n\\leq2[s(r)+\\theta(s(r))]\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nuniformly. Interior convergence gives uniform convergence on closures. Since \\(r_q\\to r\\in(0,1)\\), the radial source-annulus identifications and inverses have uniformly bounded Lipschitz constants and tend to the identity.</p><p>A finite simple slit tip has exactly one prime end above it: in the straight chart, nested crosscuts around its endpoint identify one approach class. Every limit of a cap mark’s inverse image maps to that tip, so uniform boundary convergence identifies its prime-end limit. A root may have two preimages, but no switch is placed there. A marked base point away from roots also has a unique preimage. Distinct marks remain distinct prime ends, preserving nondegenerate sign arcs.</p><p>Finally apply the correlation convergence cited as [4, Theorem 1.3], with its marked-domain conventions and prime-boundary conformal pullback for slit domains. The cited rough-boundary local normalizations cancel in correlation ratios, and bulk convergence is uniform on compact sets.</p><p>Continuity of continuum means follows by the paper’s diagonal argument: if it failed along continuum outlines, choose lattice approximations sufficiently fine that normalized means approximate those continuum means with vanishing error and the marked domains also approximate the limiting domain. The combined lattice sequence contradicts the same bulk convergence theorem.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Topological slit chart — original Lemma 8.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Topological slit chart — original Lemma 8.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let a Jordan base have finitely many disjoint attached simple arcs at distinct roots, with a fixed compact target disjoint from the graph.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A neighborhood homeomorphism makes the base a regular circular line and the attached arcs disjoint straight segments on its fresh side, with an open collar beyond every tip. The construction may be confined to a neighborhood disjoint from the target.</p>", "proof": "<p>Subdivide the base at roots and add vertices so the graph is a finite simple plane graph. The cited planar embedding theorem [13, Theorem 1], with isomorphism defined by a plane homeomorphism, gives a polygonal embedding by an ambient homeomorphism. Each free tip then has a neighborhood where it is a straight half-edge and can be prolonged.</p><p>The Jordan–Schoenflies extension theorem cited as [11, Section 10, Theorem 4] supplies a base collar. Prolong slits to a surrounding fresh-side curve, choosing prolongations disjoint. An arc attached at only one boundary endpoint does not separate the adjacent open region, so its free endpoint can be connected to the surrounding curve without crossing the other attached arcs. Simplify each connecting path; cut along a chosen prolongation and repeat in the appropriate Jordan region. Choose distinct surrounding endpoints in root cyclic order.</p><p>The prolonged arcs cut the collar into finitely many Jordan cells. Map their boundaries compatibly to rectangular cells in a circular strip, with tips at interior points of corresponding straight sides. Extend in each cell by the same plane extension theorem. The finitely many extensions glue continuously with continuous inverse.</p><p>Choose the neighborhood and prolongations disjoint from the target; compactness supplies a final positive clearance. No common metric modulus is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Small regularization of outlines — original Lemma 8.3.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Small regularization of outlines — original Lemma 8.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let a Jordan outline have finitely many marked points and a sufficiently small surrounding Jordan collar.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a polygonal or smooth replacement through an ambient homeomorphism supported in that collar, with arbitrarily small displacement. Marks retain cyclic order and may lie on straight grid-aligned portions. Finitely many disjoint outlines may be treated simultaneously.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use a Schoenflies parametrization of a closed annular collar. Uniform continuity gives thin, small quadrilateral strips with small overlaps between consecutive strips and disjoint nonconsecutive strips. Put marks away from overlaps.</p><p>Choose joining points inside overlaps. Join successive points by polygonal arcs in their open strip, erasing loops. In each overlap, truncate adjacent arcs before their first intersection and join them by a simple polygonal bridge. Operations are local in disjoint overlaps. At marked strips insert short horizontal or vertical segments and place the marks there. Smooth corners away from marks if desired.</p><p>Choose transverse crosscuts in the overlaps, giving corresponding old and new Jordan cells above and below the outlines. Map outer boundaries identically, middle arcs to their replacements, and matching crosscuts compatibly. Extend cellwise by the plane extension theorem. The maps glue to a homeomorphism equal to the identity outside the collar. Corresponding cells lie in the same small strip neighborhoods, so both forward and inverse displacement are arbitrarily small.</p><p>Use disjoint collars for simultaneous treatment.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Boundary-incidence ordering and examined-wall signs — original Lemmas 8.5–8.6.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Boundary-incidence ordering and examined-wall signs — original Lemmas 8.5–8.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Represent compared ferromagnetic Ising laws on a common finite nearest-neighbor graph by pinning vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C\\) be a target-containing component of vertices free in both laws.</p><p class=\"li\">For stopping-wall applications, fix an admissible limiting datum, disjoint small neighborhoods of its roots and tips, sufficiently close resolved data, and sufficiently fine mesh.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. For two laws \\(\\mu_0,\\mu_1\\), if every edge \\(xy\\) with \\(x\\in C\\), \\(y\\notin C\\), satisfies at least one of\n   \\[\n   y\\text{ is pinned minus in model }0,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y\\text{ is pinned plus in model }1,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y\\text{ is pinned identically in both models},\n   \\]\n   then\n   \\[\n   \\mu_0|_C\\preceq\\mu_1|_C.\n   \\]\n   The same holds for unions of target components with the corresponding incidence condition.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Outside the node neighborhoods, an edge from a free target-component vertex to an examined neighbor sees the sign of the corresponding accessible face of the limiting datum. Its approach to the realized wall has physical length \\(O(\\delta)\\); identification with the limiting face also uses the datum tolerance and fixed chart’s modulus.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 10.1 proves the discrete ordering criterion; its proof applies componentwise to a union. Lemma 10.2 identifies the examined signs needed to verify that criterion for stopping walls.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Ordering by incidences — original Lemma 8.5.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Ordering by incidences — original Lemma 8.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the two laws, common free component \\(C\\), and three incidence alternatives in Proposition 10.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mu_0|_C\\preceq\\mu_1|_C.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Condition separately on all spins outside \\(C\\). A boundary neighbor cannot be free in both models, since its edge would place it in the common free component. Each alternative gives\n\\[\n\\sigma_0(y)\\leq\\sigma_1(y)\n\\]\nfor every pair of exterior assignments compatible with the pins.</p><p>Thus model \\(0\\)’s induced boundary field at every vertex of \\(C\\) is at most model \\(1\\)’s. Internal ferromagnetic couplings agree. A monotone finite heat-bath coupling, equivalently boundary monotonicity, gives domination for every such pair of exterior assignments. Mixing proves the marginal statement.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Examined triangles preserve the facing sign — original Lemma 8.6.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Examined triangles preserve the facing sign — original Lemma 8.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the fixed limiting datum, excluded root and tip neighborhoods, close resolved walls, and adaptive reveal of Proposition 10.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every free-to-examined incidence accessible from the target, outside those neighborhoods, carries the corresponding limiting accessible-face sign.</p>", "proof": "<p>An edge between free vertices cannot cross a traced zero segment: the traversed triangle containing the crossing would have caused both endpoints to be examined. The same applies to the open part of an edge ending at an examined vertex.</p><p>If that endpoint was inspected in a traversed triangle, append an \\(O(\\delta)\\) approach inside the triangle on the zero-segment side bearing its spin sign. Affine interpolation gives a constant vertex sign on each side. If it belongs to the initial reveal, approach the accessible starting-polygon arc directly. Every initial sign switch creates a starting-contour zero crossing and is explored, so no extra switch occurs on a retained base arc away from roots.</p><p>Outside the node neighborhoods, the limiting edge lies in a chart strip separated from nonincident pieces. Parametrized convergence places the perturbed strand in a narrow middle substrip as a crosscut between plugged ends. A simple crosscut separates the long sides even if it backtracks repeatedly. The target-side approach cannot change sides without crossing the wall, so its sign is the accessible face sign.</p><p>Fixed positive physical separation absorbs the \\(O(\\delta)\\) detour. Going around an untraced end is possible only near a stopping tip, which has been excluded.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**A common weight for a distant wall — original Lemma 8.7.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**A common weight for a distant wall — original Lemma 8.7.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C,W\\) be nested lattice square contours with a fixed positive macroscopic critical square-lattice collar.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\) lie strictly inside \\(C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\nu_1,\\nu_2\\) have plus pins on \\(W\\), possibly different prescribed spins inside \\(C\\), but identical interactions and pinning rule on and outside \\(C\\), where \\(C\\) is free.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\nu_i^b\\) replace the plus wall by any assignment \\(b\\) on \\(W\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a positive function \\(w_b(\\sigma_C)\\), independent of all data strictly inside \\(C\\), such that\n\\[\nd\\nu_i^b|_{T\\cup C}\n=\n\\frac{w_b\\,d\\nu_i|_{T\\cup C}}{\\nu_i(w_b)},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{\\sup w_b}{\\inf w_b}\\leq K,\n\\]\nuniformly in mesh and \\(b\\), with \\(K\\) depending only on the collar. Therefore\n\\[\n\\|\\nu_1^b|_T-\\nu_2^b|_T\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n\\leq2K\\|\\nu_1|_{T\\cup C}-\\nu_2|_{T\\cup C}\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For contour assignment \\(j\\), sum spins strictly between \\(C,W\\), with \\(j\\) on \\(C\\) and \\(b\\) on \\(W\\), obtaining an exterior partition weight \\(A_b(j)\\). Allocate energies along \\(C\\) identically in all laws. Then\n\\[\nw_b(j)=A_b(j)/A_+(j)\n\\]\nis independent of interior data. Factorization at \\(C\\) gives the common-weight identity, with separate normalizations.</p><p>In the collar law with both contours free, the joint weight of \\((j,b)\\) is proportional to \\(A_b(j)\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\log w_b(j)-\\log w_b(j')\n=\n\\log A_b(j)+\\log A_+(j')\n-\\log A_+(j)-\\log A_b(j')\n\\]\nis a log-odds rectangle. Proposition 1 and the fixed-collar circuit bound of Proposition 2 bound its absolute value by a geometric constant. Wall-only energies contribute scalars that cancel from this oscillation.</p><p>Rescale so \\(1\\leq w_b\\leq K\\), and put\n\\[\nd=\\|\\nu_1|_{T\\cup C}-\\nu_2|_{T\\cup C}\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}.\n\\]\nLayer cake gives, for every joint event \\(B\\),\n\\[\n|\\nu_1(w_b\\mathbf1_B)-\\nu_2(w_b\\mathbf1_B)|\\leq Kd,\n\\quad\n|\\nu_1(w_b)-\\nu_2(w_b)|\\leq Kd.\n\\]\nUsing normalizers at least one,\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\frac{\\nu_1(w_b\\mathbf1_B)}{\\nu_1(w_b)}\n-\n\\frac{\\nu_2(w_b\\mathbf1_B)}{\\nu_2(w_b)}\n\\right|\\leq2Kd.\n\\]\nTake the supremum and restrict to \\(T\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Local ordered erosion and displacement of completed barriers — original Lemmas 8.1 and 8.8.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Local ordered erosion and displacement of completed barriers — original Lemmas 8.1 and 8.8.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix an admissible facing datum \\(d\\), an inward or outward band, and a buffered compact target beyond the band.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a free source contour \\(S_0\\) and square test contour \\(J\\) between the whole permitted band and the target. Give them fixed positive collars so \\(J\\) is admissible for Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">All boundary modifications occur on the band side of \\(S_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  M_J^\\delta=\\delta^{7/8}\\sum_{x\\in J^\\delta}\\sigma_x.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">In the outward case, the distant wall is unchanged, but its prescribed signs may be arbitrary.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), there are an open neighborhood \\(U_d\\subseteq X_{\\mathrm{band}}\\), a regular signed Jordan contour \\(\\Gamma_d\\) on the fresh side, and \\(\\delta_d&gt;0\\), such that every realizable transcript with datum in \\(U_d\\), at every \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;\\delta_d\\), has the following properties.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The lattice layer following \\(\\Gamma_d\\) is free in the original conditional law \\(\\mu^0\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Inserting its plus pins gives \\(\\mu^+\\), and then inserting its minus pins gives \\(\\mu^f\\). The first change is upward, the second downward, and\n   \\[\n   \\|\\mu^0|_T-\\mu^+|_T\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n   +\n   \\|\\mu^+|_T-\\mu^f|_T\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n   &lt;\\varepsilon.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. On the fresh side, \\(\\mu^f\\) is precisely the Gibbs law specified by \\(\\Gamma_d\\), the mesh, and the unchanged distant wall, independent of the transcript.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. The contour may be polygonal or smooth, with switches on smooth portions. Its clearances, neighborhood, and mesh threshold do not depend on remaining conditional spins or the transcript. Choices are uniform over distant-wall assignments.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. In the inward case, the whole free component inside \\(\\Gamma_d\\), together with a positive exterior collar, contains no old pins.</p><p>Additionally, let a regular signed contour \\(\\Gamma\\) be fully pinned in the inward setting, let \\(V^\\delta\\) be its entire enclosed free component with the pinned layer excluded, and assume an exterior collar has no other pins. For every buffered inner target and \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), a regular surrounding contour \\(\\widehat\\Gamma\\) with corresponding signs can be fixed at positive distance so that, with its pins present from the start:</p><p class=\"li\">removing old plus pins changes the target law by less than \\(\\varepsilon\\), downward;</p><p class=\"li\">then removing old minus pins changes it by less than \\(\\varepsilon\\), upward.</p><p>Initially the farther pins do not affect \\(V^\\delta\\). The same kept volume is retained throughout, and after both removals it has positive macroscopic distance from every fixed pin.</p>", "proof": "<p>First reduce an arbitrary outward wall to a constant plus wall. Choose a square \\(C\\) between the compact target \\(T\\) and distant wall \\(W\\), enclosing the whole permitted band. Apply the constant-wall construction to \\(T\\cup C\\), with total error below \\(\\varepsilon/(2K)\\). This enlarged target has the required clearances. Proposition 11 transfers the two joint errors to \\(T\\) under every assignment \\(b\\) on \\(W\\), with the same weight for every transcript and both insertions. Ordered insertion remains ordered under the prescribed wall. Finite Gibbs weights are positive, so every interior transcript is realizable under the plus reference wall. It therefore suffices to prove the constant-wall assertion.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nm_j=\\mathbb E_{\\mu^j}M_J^\\delta,\\qquad j\\in\\{0,+,f\\}.\n\\]\nPlus insertion is upward and subsequent minus insertion downward, so \\(m_+\\geq m_0,m_f\\). All changed pins precede the common free source \\(S_0\\); Proposition 6 gives\n\\[\n\\|\\mu^0|_T-\\mu^+|_T\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n+\n\\|\\mu^+|_T-\\mu^f|_T\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n\\leq C[(m_+-m_0)+(m_+-m_f)].\n\\]</p><p>Choose plug sizes first by Lemma 12.1, making each law’s total plug error less than \\(a\\). Next choose \\(q\\) using Lemma 12.2, then \\(\\rho\\), regularization, neighborhood, and mesh using Lemma 12.3.</p><p>Removing plugs from the sandwich bounds each \\(m_j\\) between the two unmodified regular means, with error at most \\(2a\\) at either end. Each ordered mean difference is therefore at most the absolute regular mean gap plus \\(4a\\). Choose \\(a\\), then \\(q\\), to make the total-variation sum less than \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>The completed layer separates the target from every old examined vertex. Gibbs factorization leaves precisely the law with the new contour and unchanged distant wall. Omitted initial spins and nonvisible traces are blocked. A numerical stopping radius with no visible tip adds no boundary piece. Thus the completed law remains transcript-independent even when numerical radii are omitted from the datum; averaging omitted information preserves the bounds.</p><p>For displacement, original Lemma 8.8, place a sufficiently thin outward signed offset \\(\\widehat\\Gamma\\) before changing \\(\\Gamma\\). The full old layer initially disconnects \\(V^\\delta\\), so farther pins have no effect.</p><p>Apply the same no-slit construction with plugs around the finitely many sign switches and upper and lower outlines moving plus and minus arcs in their ordered directions. The test contour lies strictly inside \\(\\Gamma\\), with a free collar. Proposition 9 makes envelope means approach the same limit as offset vanishes; Proposition 2 makes plug errors vanish. Proposition 10 sandwiches the original law, the law with only old minus pins, and the law with no old pins. Proposition 6 controls the two ordered removals.</p><p>Retain the same \\(V^\\delta\\); do not discard an inner free strip. Fix a sufficiently small positive offset after achieving the target estimates. Mesh rounding preserves a fixed fraction of its distance. Effective pins are then on the farther contour, and original pins lie beyond the available collar, proving the final buffer.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Small node plugs have vanishing magnetic cost — equation (8.8) in the proof of original Lemma 8.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Small node plugs have vanishing magnetic cost — equation (8.8) in the proof of original Lemma 8.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed source and test contours of Proposition 12.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose finitely many disjoint small closed chart-rectangle neighborhoods \\(P_1,\\ldots,P_k\\) of roots and tips, adding an auxiliary base node if needed.</p><p class=\"li\">Inside each choose a smaller rectangle, leaving positive joining slack; incident ideal edges are monotone near nodes in their separate strips.</p><p class=\"li\">A plugged law overwrites every spin in each \\(P_i\\) with a common assignment in every compared model.</p><p class=\"li\">All compared laws retain the fixed free neighborhood of \\(J\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For a single plug \\(P\\) and any compared law \\(\\lambda\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\mathbb E_\\lambda M_J^\\delta-\n\\mathbb E_{\\lambda^P}M_J^\\delta\n\\right|\n\\leq\\omega(\\operatorname{diam}P),\n\\qquad \\omega(t)\\to0,\n\\]\nuniformly in other pins and sufficiently fine meshes. The total cost of finitely many plugs can be made smaller than any prescribed \\(a&gt;0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Terminal symmetry in Proposition 1 and the small-patch comparison of Proposition 2 control the remote \\(J^\\delta\\)-density when an assignment in \\(P\\) changes. If \\(R_P\\) is that density ratio,\n\\[\ne^{-b_P}\\leq R_P\\leq e^{b_P},\n\\qquad b_P\\to0\n\\]\nas physical plug diameter tends to zero.</p><p>Proposition 3 bounds the second moment of \\(M_J^\\delta\\), so\n\\[\n\\left|\\mathbb E_\\lambda[(R_P-1)M_J^\\delta]\\right|\n\\leq(e^{b_P}-1)\n\\bigl(\\mathbb E_\\lambda(M_J^\\delta)^2\\bigr)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nIf spins in \\(P\\) were free, condition on their values, apply the assignment comparison, and average. To overwrite pins, first remove their prescriptions while retaining vertices and edges, and use the same comparison. Other pins remain common.</p><p>No free collar is needed around \\(P\\). Sum over the finitely many plugs and choose their sizes accordingly.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Regular upper and lower magnetic envelopes — equation (8.9) in original Lemma 8.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Regular upper and lower magnetic envelopes — equation (8.9) in original Lemma 8.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the slit chart and fixed node plugs of Lemma 12.1.</p><p class=\"li\">For this analytic step, the outward distant wall is constant.</p><p class=\"li\">For small chart displacement \\(q&gt;0\\), form an upper regular outline by moving a plus base face toward the fresh side and a minus base face away from it.</p><p class=\"li\">On a slit, move a thin ribbon entirely toward its plus side by distance comparable to \\(q\\), retaining both face signs and thickness a small fixed fraction of \\(q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Reverse these moves for a lower outline.</p><p class=\"li\">Join inside smaller node rectangles by fixed scaled templates, with one cap switch and no narrowing neck. Regularize within a smaller fraction of all clearances.</p><p class=\"li\">Denote the resulting unmodified laws by \\(\\nu_q^u,\\nu_q^l\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\lim_{q\\downarrow0}\\limsup_{\\delta\\downarrow0}\n\\left|\n\\mathbb E_{\\nu_q^u}M_J^\\delta-\n\\mathbb E_{\\nu_q^l}M_J^\\delta\n\\right|=0.\n\\]\nThus, after plug sizes are fixed, a sufficiently small fixed \\(q\\) makes the regular mean gap small for all sufficiently fine meshes, while all joins remain strictly inside their plugs.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 9 makes the continuum one-point functions of the upper and lower outlines converge to the same slit-domain function.</p><p>For fixed \\(q\\), the cited bulk spin-correlation convergence [4, Theorem 1.3] gives convergence of normalized lattice means uniformly on \\(J\\). Since \\(J^\\delta\\) contains \\(O(\\delta^{-1})\\) vertices and the normalization is \\(\\delta^{7/8}\\), these one-point limits yield the displayed iterated-limit identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**A uniformly free erosion layer and incidence sandwich — the geometric step in original Lemma 8.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**A uniformly free erosion layer and incidence sandwich — the geometric step in original Lemma 8.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed plugs and upper and lower outlines of Lemmas 12.1–12.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(0&lt;\\rho\\ll q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Construct an outline at fresh-side chart distance \\(\\rho\\), parallel to the base and around both sides and the tip of each slit, with face signs and one cap switch.</p><p class=\"li\">Regularize it to \\(\\Gamma_d\\), placing switches on grid-aligned straight portions.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mu^0,\\mu^+,\\mu^f\\) be the original, plus-inserted, and fully inserted transcript laws.</p><p class=\"li\">Superscript \\(P\\) denotes overwriting all node plugs with the same assignments.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After choosing a sufficiently small datum neighborhood and then a sufficiently fine mesh:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The erosion layer is free in \\(\\mu^0\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. In the inward case, its entire enclosed free component and a positive exterior collar have no old pins.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. On the common target component,\n   \\[\n   (\\nu_q^l)^P\\preceq(\\mu^j)^P\\preceq(\\nu_q^u)^P,\n   \\qquad j\\in\\{0,+,f\\}.\n   \\]</p>", "proof": "<p>After fixing \\(\\rho\\) and regularization, the new layer has positive physical separation from the ideal datum. Choose the datum tolerance and mesh to preserve this separation. Undoing the recording map moves curves by \\(O(\\delta)\\); uniform continuity of the chart and its inverse converts recorded tolerance and fine mesh into the required physical slack. Absorb examined triangles’ \\(O(\\delta)\\) thickness.</p><p>Inwardly, if \\(D\\) is the Jordan interior with slits removed, the outline wraps around every slit and has a thin exterior collar whose union with its closed interior is compactly contained in \\(D\\). Parametrized base convergence preserves winding number uniformly on this compact set, and nearby slits stay away. Nonvisible traces and hidden pockets lie in the blocked complement. Examined vertices are on the initial side or within \\(O(\\delta)\\) of traced walls, so the kept component and collar have no old pins.</p><p>For the sandwich, consider a slit strip outside plugs, with the ideal slit at coordinate zero, plus on the left, minus on the right. The perturbed strand lies in \\(|x|&lt;\\xi\\), where\n\\[\n\\xi\\ll\\rho\\ll q.\n\\]\nThe upper ribbon is near \\(-q\\), erosion plus layer near \\(-\\rho\\), and erosion minus layer near \\(\\rho\\).</p><p>Parametrized convergence controls visits to strip ends. On a perturbed edge, select its last exit from the first smaller node rectangle before its first entry into the second. This is a simple crosscut. Monotonicity of the ideal edge near nodes and positive slack keep discarded prefixes and suffixes inside their plugs. For example, with smaller-rectangle exit coordinate \\(2h\\), plug exit \\(3h\\), and tolerance \\(e&lt;h/3\\), the discarded prefix stays at coordinate at most \\(2h+2e&lt;3h\\).</p><p>Keep the whole selected crosscut, including later outer-plug visits. Every original edge portion outside actual plugs is retained. Filling plugs cannot join the crosscut’s two sides. One tolerance works for the finitely many edges, and fine mesh prevents passing between a wall end and its plug.</p><p>The upper ribbon, old-wall crosscut, and common root and tip plugs close their interwall channel. It cannot meet the common free target component; extra examined pieces cannot reopen it. Accessible incidences therefore see the upper plus face or the old minus face. In the fully inserted law the tested frontier may be the erosion minus layer; in the plus-only law the extra plus pins lie in the screened channel. Thus the upper comparison works for all three laws.</p><p>Proposition 10 identifies accessible examined-neighbor signs. Plug incidences agree. On a plus base arc the upper outline is nearer the fresh side and supplies a plus pin; on a minus base arc the tested wall is nearer and supplies a minus pin. Joins lie inside common plugs. The incidence criterion gives the upper domination.</p><p>For the lower outline, move the ribbon to the minus side. Accessible incidences are a lower minus face or tested plus face. Tested plus pins reinforce the order; erosion minus pins are screened. Reverse the base argument and apply the same criterion.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 3, "cite"], [1, 4, "cite"], [3, 4, "cite"], [5, 0, "cite"], [5, 8, "cite"], [5, 32, "cite"], [5, 34, "cite"], [6, 5, "cite"], [7, 5, "cite"], [8, 2, "cite"], [8, 4, "cite"], [8, 10, "cite"], [8, 21, "cite"], [8, 32, "cite"], [8, 33, "cite"], [8, 34, "cite"], [9, 5, "cite"], [9, 6, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [10, 2, "cite"], [10, 18, "cite"], [10, 34, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 10, "cite"], [13, 17, "cite"], [13, 19, "cite"], [13, 21, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 13, "cite"], [16, 14, "cite"], [17, 21, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [20, 33, "cite"], [21, 20, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [22, 25, "cite"], [23, 1, "cite"], [23, 3, "cite"], [23, 4, "cite"], [24, 23, "cite"], [24, 33, "cite"], [24, 34, "cite"], [24, 35, "cite"], [24, 36, "cite"], [25, 23, "cite"], [25, 26, "cite"], [25, 27, "cite"], [26, 33, "cite"], [26, 35, "cite"], [27, 26, "cite"], [28, 26, "cite"], [29, 33, "cite"], [29, 36, "cite"], [30, 29, "cite"], [31, 29, "cite"], [32, 26, "cite"], [32, 33, "cite"], [33, 1, "cite"], [33, 3, "cite"], [33, 4, "cite"], [34, 33, "cite"], [34, 35, "cite"], [34, 36, "cite"], [35, 33, "cite"], [35, 36, "cite"], [36, 33, "cite"]]});
